    ('q-pl90-fib-38', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Horseshoe crab blood', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Horseshoe crabs have blue blood, coloured by [[b1]]. Their blood contains cells that react to tiny amounts of bacterial toxins, so it is used to test whether medicines and medical [[b2]] are safe. The crabs are collected, partly drained of blood and returned to the sea, but some do not [[b3]], which has encouraged the development of synthetic alternatives.', 'Horseshoe crabs have blue blood, coloured by copper. Their blood contains cells that react to tiny amounts of bacterial toxins, so it is used to test whether medicines and medical equipment are safe. The crabs are collected, partly drained of blood and returned to the sea, but some do not survive, which has encouraged the development of synthetic alternatives.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "copper"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "equipment"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "survive"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: copper, equipment, survive.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-39', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Why popcorn pops', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'A popcorn kernel contains a small amount of water inside a hard outer shell. When the kernel is heated, this water turns to [[b1]], and the [[b2]] inside rises until the shell suddenly breaks. The soft starch then expands into a [[b3]] and quickly sets, turning the kernel inside out.', 'A popcorn kernel contains a small amount of water inside a hard outer shell. When the kernel is heated, this water turns to steam, and the pressure inside rises until the shell suddenly breaks. The soft starch then expands into a foam and quickly sets, turning the kernel inside out.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "steam"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "pressure"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "foam"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: steam, pressure, foam.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-40', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Why bread goes stale', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Stale bread feels dry, but staling is not mainly caused by bread losing [[b1]]. As bread cools after baking, its starch molecules slowly rearrange into a more [[b2]] structure, making the crumb firm. This process happens fastest at refrigerator temperatures, which is why bread kept in the [[b3]] goes stale sooner than bread kept at room temperature.', 'Stale bread feels dry, but staling is not mainly caused by bread losing water. As bread cools after baking, its starch molecules slowly rearrange into a more crystalline structure, making the crumb firm. This process happens fastest at refrigerator temperatures, which is why bread kept in the fridge goes stale sooner than bread kept at room temperature.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "water"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "crystalline"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "fridge"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: water, crystalline, fridge.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-41', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Gritting in winter', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Salt is spread on icy roads because it lowers the [[b1]] at which water freezes, so ice melts even when the air is below zero. However, it becomes much less effective in very severe cold. Road salt also has costs: it causes [[b2]] on cars and bridges, and when it washes into streams and soil it can harm plants and freshwater [[b3]].', 'Salt is spread on icy roads because it lowers the temperature at which water freezes, so ice melts even when the air is below zero. However, it becomes much less effective in very severe cold. Road salt also has costs: it causes rust on cars and bridges, and when it washes into streams and soil it can harm plants and freshwater wildlife.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "temperature"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "rust"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "wildlife"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: temperature, rust, wildlife.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-42', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'The vacuum flask', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'The vacuum flask was invented in 1892 by the Scottish scientist James Dewar, who needed to keep chemicals very [[b1]]. It has two walls with a vacuum between them. Because there is almost no air in the gap, heat cannot easily travel by [[b2]] or convection, and a [[b3]] coating on the walls reflects heat radiation. A German company later sold the design for everyday use.', 'The vacuum flask was invented in 1892 by the Scottish scientist James Dewar, who needed to keep chemicals very cold. It has two walls with a vacuum between them. Because there is almost no air in the gap, heat cannot easily travel by conduction or convection, and a silver coating on the walls reflects heat radiation. A German company later sold the design for everyday use.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "cold"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "conduction"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "silver"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: cold, conduction, silver.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-43', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Glow sticks', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'A glow stick contains two chemicals kept apart by a thin glass [[b1]]. Bending the stick breaks the tube, and the chemicals mix, releasing energy as [[b2]] rather than heat, a process called chemiluminescence. A dye controls the colour. Placing a glow stick in the [[b3]] slows the reaction, so it shines more dimly but lasts much longer.', 'A glow stick contains two chemicals kept apart by a thin glass tube. Bending the stick breaks the tube, and the chemicals mix, releasing energy as light rather than heat, a process called chemiluminescence. A dye controls the colour. Placing a glow stick in the freezer slows the reaction, so it shines more dimly but lasts much longer.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "tube"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "light"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "freezer"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: tube, light, freezer.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-44', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'A siren''s changing pitch', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'When an ambulance passes you, its siren seems to drop in [[b1]]. This is the Doppler effect, described by the Austrian physicist Christian Doppler in 1842. As the source moves towards you, the sound waves are squeezed closer together; as it moves away, they are [[b2]]. The same principle is used in police speed guns, weather [[b3]] and medical ultrasound.', 'When an ambulance passes you, its siren seems to drop in pitch. This is the Doppler effect, described by the Austrian physicist Christian Doppler in 1842. As the source moves towards you, the sound waves are squeezed closer together; as it moves away, they are stretched. The same principle is used in police speed guns, weather radar and medical ultrasound.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "pitch"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "stretched"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "radar"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: pitch, stretched, radar.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-45', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Golf ball dimples', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Early golf balls were smooth, but players noticed that old, [[b1]] balls flew further. Modern balls therefore have hundreds of small dimples. These create a thin layer of turbulent air around the ball, which helps the airflow cling to its surface and reduces the [[b2]] behind it. The result is less [[b3]], so a dimpled ball can travel roughly twice as far as a smooth one.', 'Early golf balls were smooth, but players noticed that old, scuffed balls flew further. Modern balls therefore have hundreds of small dimples. These create a thin layer of turbulent air around the ball, which helps the airflow cling to its surface and reduces the wake behind it. The result is less drag, so a dimpled ball can travel roughly twice as far as a smooth one.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "scuffed"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "wake"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "drag"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: scuffed, wake, drag.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-46', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Running out of glycogen', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Marathon runners sometimes describe hitting the wall, usually after about thirty kilometres. At that point, the body''s stores of [[b1]], a form of carbohydrate held in the muscles and liver, are running low. The body must rely more on [[b2]], which releases energy more slowly, so the runner feels exhausted. Eating carbohydrates before and during the race helps [[b3]] this moment.', 'Marathon runners sometimes describe hitting the wall, usually after about thirty kilometres. At that point, the body''s stores of glycogen, a form of carbohydrate held in the muscles and liver, are running low. The body must rely more on fat, which releases energy more slowly, so the runner feels exhausted. Eating carbohydrates before and during the race helps delay this moment.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "glycogen"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "fat"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "delay"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: glycogen, fat, delay.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-47', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Streaks in basketball', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Many sports fans believe in the hot hand: the idea that a player who has scored several times in a row is more likely to score [[b1]]. A famous study of basketball in 1985 found no [[b2]] for this and called it a cognitive illusion. However, later researchers discovered a subtle [[b3]] in the statistical method, and the debate has reopened.', 'Many sports fans believe in the hot hand: the idea that a player who has scored several times in a row is more likely to score again. A famous study of basketball in 1985 found no evidence for this and called it a cognitive illusion. However, later researchers discovered a subtle error in the statistical method, and the debate has reopened.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "again"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "evidence"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "error"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: again, evidence, error.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-48', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Two-factor security', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Two-factor authentication protects an account by asking for two different kinds of evidence: something you know, such as a [[b1]], and something you have, such as your [[b2]]. Even if criminals steal the password, they cannot log in without the second factor. Security experts generally consider codes from an authentication app safer than codes sent by text [[b3]].', 'Two-factor authentication protects an account by asking for two different kinds of evidence: something you know, such as a password, and something you have, such as your phone. Even if criminals steal the password, they cannot log in without the second factor. Security experts generally consider codes from an authentication app safer than codes sent by text message.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "password"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "phone"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "message"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: password, phone, message.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-49', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Ten thousand steps', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'The popular goal of walking ten thousand steps a day did not come from medical research. It began as a [[b1]] idea in Japan in the 1960s, when a company sold a step counter whose name meant ten-thousand-step [[b2]]. More recent studies suggest that health benefits increase with more steps but begin to [[b3]] off at lower totals for many people, especially older adults.', 'The popular goal of walking ten thousand steps a day did not come from medical research. It began as a marketing idea in Japan in the 1960s, when a company sold a step counter whose name meant ten-thousand-step meter. More recent studies suggest that health benefits increase with more steps but begin to level off at lower totals for many people, especially older adults.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "marketing"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "meter"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "level"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: marketing, meter, level.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-fib-50', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-fib', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks', 'Edmond Albius and vanilla', 'You will hear a recording. Type the missing words in each blank.', 'Vanilla comes from an orchid native to Mexico, where it is pollinated by local [[b1]]. When the plant was grown elsewhere, it flowered but produced almost no pods. In 1841, Edmond Albius, a twelve-year-old enslaved boy on the island of Réunion, developed a quick way to pollinate each flower by [[b2]]. His method is still used today, and the intensive [[b3]] it requires helps explain why vanilla is so expensive.', 'Vanilla comes from an orchid native to Mexico, where it is pollinated by local bees. When the plant was grown elsewhere, it flowered but produced almost no pods. In 1841, Edmond Albius, a twelve-year-old enslaved boy on the island of Réunion, developed a quick way to pollinate each flower by hand. His method is still used today, and the intensive labour it requires helps explain why vanilla is so expensive.', 7, 120, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "b1", "correctAnswer": "bees"}, {"id": "b2", "correctAnswer": "hand"}, {"id": "b3", "correctAnswer": "labour"}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The recording says: bees, hand, labour.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Listening Fill in the Blanks']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Countdown timers at crossings', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Many cities have installed countdown timers at pedestrian crossings, showing how many seconds remain before the lights change. The idea is simple: if people know exactly how much time they have, they can decide whether it is safe to start crossing, and fewer will be caught in the middle of the road when traffic starts moving. Several studies have found that pedestrians generally like the timers and feel safer with them. However, the timers can also be seen by drivers. Some research suggests that drivers approaching a junction use the countdown to judge whether they can get through before the light turns red, and some of them speed up. For this reason, a few cities have experimented with timers that are visible only to pedestrians, or have combined them with longer crossing times. The overall effect on safety appears to depend on the design of the junction.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Countdown timers help pedestrians judge whether they have time to cross, and many people feel safer with them. However, drivers can also see the timers and may speed up, so their effect on safety depends on how junctions are designed."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Countdown timers have been shown to eliminate accidents at junctions completely, because both drivers and pedestrians slow down as soon as they see the number of seconds remaining before the lights change."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Most pedestrians dislike countdown timers because they create pressure to hurry, so many cities have removed them from busy junctions and replaced them with longer crossing times and traditional signals instead."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Countdown timers were designed mainly for drivers to help them decide when to brake, and research shows that pedestrians rarely notice the numbers, so the timers have little effect on how people cross."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. Pedestrians benefit, but drivers may use the timers to speed up. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Rules on parking spaces', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'For much of the twentieth century, many cities required every new building to include a minimum number of parking spaces. A block of flats might need at least one space per apartment, and a restaurant a certain number per table. These rules were intended to prevent a shortage of on-street parking, but critics argue that they have had costly side effects. Parking takes up valuable land and can add significantly to the cost of building homes, costs that are passed on to residents whether or not they own a car. By guaranteeing cheap or free parking everywhere, the rules may also encourage people to drive rather than walk or use public transport. In recent years, a growing number of cities have removed their minimum parking requirements. Supporters point out that this does not ban parking: developers can still build as many spaces as they think their customers want.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Cities introduced parking requirements to reduce car use, and removing them has led to bans on building new car parks in many areas, forcing residents to rely completely on public transport."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Rules requiring new buildings to include parking were meant to protect street parking, but they raise building costs and may encourage driving. Many cities are now removing them, while still allowing developers to provide parking where there is demand."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Minimum parking rules have lowered the cost of housing because developers share the price of spaces with local governments, so most cities are now making the requirements even stricter than before."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Parking requirements mainly affect restaurants, which must provide one space for every table, and critics say this has forced many small businesses in city centres to close over recent decades."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. The rules raised costs and encouraged driving, and removing them does not ban parking. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Singapore''s hawker centres', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In the 1960s and 1970s, the government of Singapore began moving thousands of street food sellers, known as hawkers, off the streets and into purpose-built food centres. The main reasons were hygiene and public health: food was being prepared without clean water or proper waste disposal. The new hawker centres provided running water, drainage and regular inspections, while allowing sellers to continue offering cheap, freshly cooked dishes. Over time, the centres became an important part of daily life, places where people of different backgrounds and incomes eat side by side. In 2020, Singapore''s hawker culture was added to UNESCO''s list of intangible cultural heritage. Yet its future faces challenges. Many hawkers are elderly, the work involves long hours for modest profits, and fewer young people are willing to take it on, so schemes have been introduced to train and support new hawkers.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Singapore banned street food in the 1960s because it was too expensive for most residents, and the new hawker centres were built mainly for tourists, who now make up most of their customers."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Hawker centres were created to preserve traditional recipes, and their recognition by UNESCO has attracted so many young people that there is now a long waiting list for anyone who wants to become a hawker."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Singapore moved street food sellers into hawker centres to improve hygiene, and the centres became central to everyday life and were recognised by UNESCO. However, ageing sellers and hard working conditions threaten their future, so programmes now support new hawkers."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The main problem with Singapore''s hawker centres today is poor hygiene, since they have no running water or drainage, and inspectors have recommended moving the food sellers back onto the streets."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Hygiene drove the move, the centres became heritage, and ageing hawkers threaten their future. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-04', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Bus bunching', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Anyone who has waited a long time for a bus, only to see two or three arrive together, has experienced bus bunching. It happens even when buses leave the depot at perfectly regular intervals. Suppose one bus is delayed slightly, perhaps by traffic. By the time it reaches the next stop, more passengers have gathered, so boarding takes longer and the bus falls further behind. Meanwhile, the bus following it finds fewer passengers waiting, moves faster, and gradually catches up. Eventually the two travel together, leaving a long gap behind them. The problem is self-reinforcing, so small delays grow over the length of a route. Transport operators use several methods to reduce it. Drivers can be asked to wait at certain stops to restore even spacing, passengers can be allowed to board through all doors, and dedicated bus lanes reduce the delays that start the process.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Buses arrive together mainly because depots send them out in groups to save money on drivers, and the speaker argues that operators should simply publish more accurate timetables to solve the problem."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Bus bunching is caused by passengers who board too slowly, and the speaker recommends banning large bags on buses and removing bus lanes, which have been shown to make delays much worse."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Although buses leave at regular intervals, traffic lights are the only cause of bunching, and operators have found that there is no practical way to keep buses evenly spaced along a route."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Buses bunch because a slightly delayed bus meets more waiting passengers and slows further, while the bus behind speeds up and catches it. Holding buses at stops, boarding through all doors and bus lanes can reduce the problem."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. A small delay grows as the delayed bus collects more passengers; operators have several remedies. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-05', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Paving you can feel', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The textured paving found at many pedestrian crossings and railway platforms was invented in Japan in the 1960s by Seiichi Miyake, who wanted to help a friend who was losing his sight. The first tiles were laid in the city of Okayama in 1967. The system uses two main patterns. Raised dots warn people with visual impairments that they are approaching a hazard, such as the edge of a platform or a road. Raised lines, running in the direction of travel, guide people along a safe route. The idea spread across Japan and then around the world, and in many countries it is now required by law in certain locations. Designers have had to balance different needs, however. The raised surfaces can be uncomfortable or difficult for some wheelchair users and people with walking difficulties, and inconsistent use of the patterns between cities can cause confusion.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Tactile paving, invented in Japan in the 1960s, uses raised dots to warn of hazards and raised lines to guide direction. It has spread worldwide, but designers must consider wheelchair users and use the patterns consistently."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Tactile paving was invented in Europe to help wheelchair users cross roads safely, and it is now used only at railway stations, where its raised lines warn people about the edge of the platform."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Tactile paving uses coloured tiles that people with poor vision can see more easily, and because every city uses exactly the same patterns, it has caused no problems for any group of users."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The first textured tiles were laid in Okayama, but the idea was rejected elsewhere in Japan because the raised surfaces were considered too expensive to install and too difficult to clean regularly."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. Dots warn and lines guide; the design must balance the needs of different users. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-06', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Monderman''s streets', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Most road safety measures add something: more signs, more barriers, more traffic lights. The Dutch traffic engineer Hans Monderman argued that in some places the opposite could work better. In several town centres in the Netherlands, he removed traffic lights, road markings, kerbs and signs, creating a shared space where cars, cyclists and pedestrians mix. His reasoning was that when drivers are unsure who has priority, they slow down and pay more attention to the people around them, making eye contact instead of watching signals. Supporters report that traffic in some of these places flows smoothly at low speeds and that the areas feel more pleasant. However, the approach has critics. Organisations representing blind and partially sighted people point out that kerbs and controlled crossings are essential for them, and that relying on eye contact excludes those who cannot make it.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Hans Monderman added extra traffic lights and barriers to Dutch town centres, showing that more signs make drivers more careful, and organisations for blind people strongly supported his approach from the start."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A Dutch engineer removed signs, lights and kerbs from some town centres, arguing that uncertainty makes drivers slow down and pay attention. Supporters praise the results, but organisations for blind people say the design excludes those who cannot rely on eye contact."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Shared space schemes separate cars from pedestrians with higher kerbs, and the speaker concludes that the design has been adopted by every city in the Netherlands without any criticism at all."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Removing signs from town centres led to much higher speeds and more accidents, so the Netherlands abandoned shared space, although cyclists and pedestrians had enjoyed the more pleasant streets for a time."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Uncertainty slows drivers, but blind people''s organisations object. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-07', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Slowing traffic in towns', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The speed of a car has a dramatic effect on what happens to a pedestrian in a collision. At low speeds, most people who are hit survive; as speed increases, the risk of death rises steeply. For this reason, many towns use traffic calming measures in residential streets. Instead of relying only on speed limits and enforcement, these measures physically encourage drivers to slow down. They include road humps, raised crossings, narrowed sections of road and bends deliberately added to straight streets. Their great advantage is that they work all the time, without police present. They do have drawbacks. Road humps can slow down ambulances and fire engines, create noise as vehicles brake and accelerate, and push traffic onto neighbouring streets. Designers have responded with alternatives such as speed cushions, which have gaps wide enough for the wheels of large emergency vehicles to pass through without slowing.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Road humps are mainly used to reduce noise in residential streets, and since police must be present for them to work, many towns have replaced them with lower speed limits instead."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Traffic calming measures have no real drawbacks, because emergency vehicles are designed to pass over road humps at full speed, and drivers almost never choose different routes to avoid them."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Because pedestrians are much more likely to die when hit at higher speeds, towns use physical measures such as humps and narrowed roads to slow traffic. These work constantly but can delay emergency vehicles, so designs such as speed cushions have been developed."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The speaker argues that the speed of a car makes little difference to pedestrian safety, and that the main purpose of traffic calming is to make residential streets look more attractive."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Speed greatly affects survival; physical measures work but have drawbacks that designers address. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-08', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Standing on both sides', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'On escalators in many cities, there is an unwritten rule: stand on one side and walk on the other. It seems efficient, but on very long escalators it may not be. In 2015 and 2016, London Underground ran a trial at Holborn station, where the escalators are unusually long. Staff asked passengers to stand on both sides and not walk. Because few people are willing to walk up a very tall escalator, the walking side had often been half empty, while queues built up at the bottom for the standing side. When everyone stood, the escalators carried significantly more people at busy times, and queues shrank. However, many passengers disliked being told not to walk, and some ignored the request. After the trial ended, most people returned to their old habits. The experiment showed that what feels fastest for an individual is not always best for the crowd.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "London Underground found that walking on escalators always moves people faster, so it introduced a permanent rule requiring passengers to walk on both sides of the escalators at every station."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The Holborn trial failed because standing on both sides created longer queues, and passengers were so pleased with the old system that the station decided never to repeat the experiment again."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The trial showed that escalators at Holborn were too short for walking, and the main conclusion was that stations should build more stairs instead of relying on escalators during busy periods."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A London trial asked passengers to stand on both sides of very long escalators. This increased the number of people carried and reduced queues, but many passengers disliked it, and most returned to the usual habit afterwards."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Standing moved more people, but passengers disliked it and went back to walking. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-09', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Memos instead of slides', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'At some companies, the most important meetings begin in silence. The online retailer Amazon became known for replacing slide presentations in senior meetings with written memos of up to about six pages. Instead of watching someone present, participants spend the first part of the meeting reading the memo quietly, and only then begin the discussion. The company''s leaders argued that full sentences and paragraphs force the writer to think more clearly, because a narrative has to explain how ideas connect, whereas bullet points on slides can hide gaps in reasoning. Reading together also ensures that everyone starts the discussion with the same information, rather than pretending to have read a document in advance. The approach has critics. Writing a good memo takes a long time, and not every decision justifies that effort. Nevertheless, the idea has been copied by other organisations looking for better decisions.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Some companies, including Amazon, replace slides with memos that everyone reads silently at the start of meetings. Supporters say full sentences force clearer thinking and give everyone the same information, although writing good memos takes a long time."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Amazon banned all written documents from its meetings because they took too long to read, and it now relies on short slide presentations with bullet points in order to speed up its decisions."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The speaker argues that silent reading at the start of meetings wastes time, since most people read documents in advance, and that slides show how ideas connect more clearly than paragraphs."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Six-page memos were introduced to reduce the number of meetings at Amazon, and critics say they make decisions too quickly because nobody has time to discuss the ideas they contain."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. Silent reading of narrative memos aims at clearer thinking, at the cost of writing time. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-10', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Ranking employees', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In the 1980s and 1990s, the American company General Electric became famous for a system of forced ranking. Managers had to place employees into categories: roughly the top twenty percent, who were rewarded, the middle seventy percent, and the bottom ten percent, who were often asked to leave. Supporters argued that the system prevented managers from giving everyone the same average rating and ensured that poor performance was addressed. Many other large companies adopted similar methods. Over time, however, criticism grew. Because employees were compared with their colleagues, the system could discourage cooperation, since helping a teammate might lower your own ranking. It could also be unfair in a strong team, where someone had to be labelled a poor performer even if everyone was doing well. Many companies, including some early adopters, have since abandoned forced ranking in favour of more frequent conversations about performance.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "General Electric introduced forced ranking to encourage teamwork, and the system proved so successful at improving cooperation that almost every large company still uses it in exactly the same form."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Forced ranking, popularised by General Electric, required managers to sort staff into fixed categories, with the lowest group often dismissed. It was widely copied, but it discouraged cooperation and could be unfair to strong teams, so many companies have dropped it."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Under forced ranking, every employee received the same average rating, which critics said hid poor performance, so companies replaced it with a system that dismissed the lowest ten percent of staff each year."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Forced ranking was abandoned because it was too expensive to run, although employees generally preferred it to regular conversations with their managers about how well they were doing in their jobs."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Fixed categories were widely copied but hurt cooperation, so many firms abandoned them. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-11', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Does brainstorming work?', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The term brainstorming was popularised in the 1950s by the advertising executive Alex Osborn, who claimed that groups following his rules, such as withholding criticism and aiming for quantity, could produce far more ideas than individuals. Later research has not supported this claim. In many experiments, people who worked alone and then pooled their ideas produced more, and often better, ideas than the same number of people brainstorming together. Psychologists have identified several reasons. In a group, only one person can speak at a time, so others may forget their ideas while waiting. Some people hold back because they worry about being judged, and some put in less effort when others can carry the load. Techniques such as brainwriting, in which people write ideas down individually before sharing them, try to keep the benefits of group discussion while avoiding these problems.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Research has confirmed Alex Osborn''s claim that groups produce more ideas than individuals, mainly because group members encourage each other to speak and nobody worries about being judged by others."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Brainstorming fails because groups criticise ideas too harshly, so psychologists recommend banning all discussion and asking a single expert to make every decision alone, without consulting any of their colleagues at all."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Although brainstorming was promoted as a way for groups to generate more ideas, research shows individuals working alone and then pooling ideas often do better. Waiting to speak and fear of judgement are reasons, and brainwriting tries to avoid these problems."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The speaker explains that brainwriting was invented in the 1950s by an advertising executive, who found that people write faster than they speak and therefore produce better ideas in groups."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Research contradicts Osborn''s claim; group problems explain why, and brainwriting responds. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-12', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Stopping the production line', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'On a Toyota production line, any worker who notices a problem can pull a cord, called an andon cord, that alerts a team leader and, if the problem cannot be solved quickly, stops the entire line. To outsiders this seems surprising. Stopping production is expensive, and many factories had traditionally tried to keep lines moving at all costs, fixing faulty products at the end. Toyota''s approach was based on the idea that problems should be solved immediately, at their source, before faults are repeated on many vehicles. It also sent a clear message that every worker was trusted and responsible for quality. The system became one of the best-known features of Toyota''s production methods. Other industries have borrowed the idea. Some hospitals, for example, encourage any member of staff, regardless of seniority, to stop a procedure if they believe a patient''s safety is at risk.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Toyota prevents workers from stopping the production line because it is too expensive, preferring to fix faulty cars at the end, and hospitals have copied this approach to avoid delays in treatment."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The andon cord allows managers to stop production when they are dissatisfied with workers, and the system was abandoned after other car companies showed that it reduced quality across their factories."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The speaker explains that the andon cord was first used in hospitals to protect patients and was later adopted by Toyota, where only senior engineers are allowed to use it on the line."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Toyota allows any worker to pull a cord that can stop the production line when a problem appears, so faults are fixed at their source. This shows trust in workers, and similar ideas have been adopted elsewhere, including in hospitals."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Any worker can stop the line so problems are fixed at once; hospitals borrowed the idea. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-13', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Alfa, Bravo, Charlie', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Over a noisy radio connection, letters such as B, D and P can sound almost identical, and a single misunderstood letter can cause serious problems, for example in an aircraft''s call sign. For this reason, pilots and air traffic controllers use a spelling alphabet in which each letter is represented by a word: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie and so on. The current version was adopted internationally in the 1950s after extensive testing. The words were chosen so that they would be clearly distinguishable from one another and easy to pronounce for speakers of different languages, not only English. Some spellings were adjusted for this reason: Alfa is written with an f, and Juliett with two t''s, so that speakers of other languages would pronounce them correctly. The alphabet is now used well beyond aviation, by the military, police, emergency services and customer service staff.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Because letters can be confused over the radio, aviation uses a spelling alphabet with a word for each letter. Adopted in the 1950s after testing, its words were chosen to be distinct and easy for speakers of many languages to pronounce."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The spelling alphabet was designed for English speakers only, and it is now used mainly in customer service because pilots prefer to spell out letters without using any code words."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Pilots once used numbers instead of letters over the radio, and the spelling alphabet was introduced in the 1990s after a series of accidents caused by confusing numbers that sounded similar."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The alphabet''s unusual spellings, such as Alfa, are mistakes that were never corrected, and experts are now considering replacing the words with ones that are easier for English speakers to remember."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. The words prevent confusion and were chosen for speakers of many languages. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-14', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The meaning of SOS', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The distress signal SOS was adopted at an international radio conference in 1906 and came into use in 1908. Many people believe the letters stand for save our souls or save our ship, but these phrases were invented afterwards. SOS was chosen because in Morse code it forms a simple, distinctive pattern: three dots, three dashes, three dots, sent as one continuous signal. It is easy to transmit and difficult to mistake for anything else, even by an operator with poor reception. Before SOS became universal, different countries used different signals, which could cause confusion. When the Titanic sank in 1912, its radio operators sent both the older signal and SOS. By the end of the twentieth century, Morse code had been replaced at sea by satellite-based distress systems, which can automatically send a ship''s position, but SOS remains widely recognised as a call for help.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "SOS originally stood for save our souls, and it was chosen by the Titanic''s radio operators in 1912 because the older distress signal had already been abolished by international agreement."}, {"id": "B", "text": "SOS was chosen as a distress signal because its Morse pattern is simple and hard to mistake, not because the letters stand for a phrase. It replaced varied national signals, and although satellites have replaced Morse at sea, SOS remains widely recognised."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The letters SOS were selected because they were the easiest to pronounce over the radio, and Morse code is still the main system that ships use to call for help at sea today."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Different countries continued to use their own distress signals until the end of the twentieth century, when satellite systems introduced SOS as the first international call for help at sea."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. The pattern, not a phrase, explains SOS; it outlived Morse code at sea. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-15', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'James Biggs''s white stick', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The white cane is recognised in many countries as a symbol of blindness, but its history is relatively recent. In 1921, James Biggs, an English photographer who had lost his sight, painted his walking stick white so that drivers would notice him more easily as traffic increased. In the 1930s, organisations in France and North America campaigned for white canes to be widely adopted, and many places passed laws requiring drivers to give way to pedestrians carrying one. The cane''s role changed significantly after the Second World War, when a rehabilitation specialist working with blinded veterans developed a technique using a longer cane. Instead of simply supporting the user or signalling to others, it was swept from side to side in front of the body to detect obstacles, steps and kerbs. This long cane technique is still taught today, alongside guide dogs and newer electronic aids.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "White canes were first introduced for soldiers blinded in the First World War, and they are now used mainly as walking supports rather than as a way of detecting obstacles in the street."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The long cane technique was developed in the 1920s by a photographer, and it has since been completely replaced by guide dogs and electronic devices in most countries around the world."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The white cane began in 1921 when a blind photographer painted his stick white to be seen by drivers. Campaigns and laws spread its use, and after the Second World War a longer cane was developed to detect obstacles, a technique still taught."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Laws in the 1930s banned white canes because drivers found them confusing, but they returned after the war when veterans campaigned for the right to use them again in public places."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. From visibility in 1921 to the long cane technique after the war. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-16', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Headphones and hearing', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Listening to music through headphones is one of the most common ways people expose themselves to loud sound. Inside the inner ear, tiny hair cells convert sound vibrations into signals for the brain. Very loud or prolonged noise can damage these cells, and in humans they do not grow back, so the resulting hearing loss is permanent. Often it develops gradually, and people may not notice until it is advanced. The risk depends on both volume and time: a moderately loud sound can be harmful if heard for many hours, while a very loud sound can cause damage quickly. The World Health Organization has warned that more than a billion young people are at risk because of unsafe listening habits. Some experts suggest a simple guideline: listen at no more than sixty percent of maximum volume for no more than sixty minutes at a time.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Hearing loss from headphones is usually temporary, because the hair cells in the inner ear grow back within a few weeks, so doctors recommend occasional breaks rather than a lower volume."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Only very loud sounds can damage hearing, while moderate volumes are safe for any length of time, so experts say young people can listen for as long as they like at medium volume."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The World Health Organization has banned headphones for young people because more than a billion have already lost their hearing completely through unsafe listening habits over the last few years."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Loud or prolonged sound through headphones can permanently damage the hair cells in the inner ear, often without people noticing. Risk depends on both volume and time, so experts suggest limits such as sixty percent volume for sixty minutes."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Damage is permanent and depends on both volume and duration. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-17', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Mediterranean eating', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The Mediterranean diet, rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains, fish and olive oil, has long been associated with good health. One of the most influential studies was a large trial in Spain involving around seven and a half thousand people at high risk of heart disease. Participants were randomly assigned either to a Mediterranean diet with extra olive oil or nuts, or to advice to follow a low-fat diet. When the results were published in 2013, the Mediterranean groups had significantly fewer heart attacks, strokes and deaths from heart disease. Later, however, researchers discovered problems with how some participants had been assigned to groups. The original paper was withdrawn in 2018 and republished with a reanalysis. The main conclusions were similar, but the authors described them more cautiously. The episode shows both the value of large trials and the importance of checking how they are carried out.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "A large Spanish trial found that people at high risk of heart disease had fewer heart problems on a Mediterranean diet. Problems with group assignment led to the paper being withdrawn and republished with similar but more cautious conclusions."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A Spanish trial showed that a low-fat diet protects the heart better than a Mediterranean diet, and the study was withdrawn because its results could not be repeated in other countries."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The Mediterranean diet trial was cancelled in 2013 after participants refused to eat olive oil and nuts, and researchers concluded that diet has no effect on the risk of heart disease."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Although the trial''s methods were flawed, the reanalysis found that the Mediterranean diet caused more strokes, so doctors now advise against it for people at high risk of heart disease."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. The benefit held up after reanalysis, but was stated more cautiously. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-18', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Cutting sodium slowly', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Eating too much salt raises blood pressure, which increases the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Much of the salt people eat is not added at the table but is already in processed foods such as bread, cereals and ready meals. In the early 2000s, the United Kingdom began a programme of salt reduction that worked with food manufacturers to lower the salt content of their products gradually. The key was to make changes slowly, a little at a time, so that customers did not notice a difference in taste and did not switch to other brands. Over the following decade, average salt consumption fell noticeably, and researchers linked this to falls in average blood pressure. Because the targets were voluntary, however, progress depended on the cooperation of the industry, and some experts believe it slowed when government pressure weakened in later years.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The United Kingdom reduced salt consumption by banning salt shakers in restaurants, since most salt is added at the table, and blood pressure fell as a result of this strict new law."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The United Kingdom worked with food manufacturers to reduce salt in processed foods gradually, so customers did not notice. Salt intake and blood pressure fell, but because targets were voluntary, progress slowed when pressure on the industry weakened."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Food manufacturers cut salt quickly and dramatically, which caused many customers to switch brands, so the government abandoned its salt reduction programme after only a few years of disappointing sales and complaints."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The salt reduction programme had no measurable effect on health, and experts believe voluntary targets are more effective than laws because companies always cooperate fully with them over long periods."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Gradual, voluntary reductions worked but slowed when pressure eased. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-19', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Fluoridation', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In the early twentieth century, a dentist in Colorado noticed that many local people had brown stains on their teeth, but surprisingly few cavities. Researchers eventually linked both effects to naturally high levels of fluoride in the water supply. Fluoride makes tooth enamel more resistant to decay, but too much can cause staining. In 1945, the city of Grand Rapids in the United States became the first to add a carefully controlled amount of fluoride to its drinking water, and rates of tooth decay among children fell. Many countries followed. The practice remains debated. Critics argue that people should be able to choose whether to consume fluoride, and that the benefit is now smaller because most toothpaste contains it. Several European countries do not fluoridate their water. However, major public health organisations continue to support fluoridation at recommended levels as a safe and cheap way to reduce tooth decay.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Fluoride was added to water in Colorado because local people had many cavities, and it has since been banned worldwide after studies showed that it causes tooth decay in children."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Grand Rapids stopped adding fluoride to its water in 1945 because of brown stains on teeth, and most European countries now require fluoride in every public water supply by law."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The link between fluoride and fewer cavities was discovered through stained teeth in Colorado, and cities began adding controlled amounts to water from 1945. The practice is debated, partly because toothpaste now contains fluoride, but health organisations still support it."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Public health organisations no longer recommend fluoridation, because toothpaste has made it unnecessary, although critics argue that governments should add even more fluoride to drinking water than they currently do."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Discovered by accident, adopted from 1945, debated today but still supported. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-20', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Risk compensation', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'When cars become safer, do drivers behave more carefully or less? A well-known study in Munich in the 1980s followed a fleet of taxis, some fitted with anti-lock brakes, which help drivers keep control when braking hard, and some without. The researchers expected the taxis with the new brakes to have fewer accidents. They did not. Observers found that drivers of the better-equipped taxis tended to follow other cars more closely and brake more sharply. This is an example of what psychologists call risk compensation: when people feel safer, they may take more risks, cancelling out some of the benefit. The idea has been used to question safety measures from seat belts to cycle helmets. However, most researchers do not conclude that safety features are useless. Rather, they argue that the real effect of a safety measure depends on how people change their behaviour in response.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Taxis fitted with anti-lock brakes had far fewer accidents in Munich, which shows that drivers become more careful when their vehicles are safer, exactly as psychologists had predicted in advance."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Risk compensation proves that seat belts, helmets and anti-lock brakes make roads more dangerous, so researchers recommend removing these features from vehicles to encourage careful driving among ordinary motorists."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The Munich study compared taxi drivers with private drivers and found that professional drivers take fewer risks, because they are trained to keep a safe distance from other vehicles at all times."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A Munich study found taxis with anti-lock brakes had no fewer accidents, because their drivers followed closer and braked harder. This illustrates risk compensation, though researchers argue safety features still matter and their effect depends on how behaviour changes."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Safer equipment led to riskier driving, yet safety features are not considered useless. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-21', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The Plimsoll line', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In the nineteenth century, many British cargo ships sailed dangerously overloaded, and hundreds were lost at sea each year. Some owners had little incentive to prevent this, because their ships and cargoes were insured, while the sailors who drowned were easily replaced. The politician Samuel Plimsoll campaigned against these so-called coffin ships, facing strong opposition from shipowners. In 1876, a law was passed requiring ships to carry a mark on their hull showing the maximum depth to which they could be loaded. This became known as the Plimsoll line. Today load lines are required by international agreement. They include several marks, because a ship floats at a different depth depending on the water: it sits lower in fresh water than in denser seawater, and extra safety margins are needed in winter seas. The simple painted line has saved countless lives.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Overloaded British ships once sank in large numbers, partly because owners were insured. After Samuel Plimsoll''s campaign, an 1876 law required a load line on hulls, and international rules now include several marks for different waters and seasons."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Samuel Plimsoll was a shipowner who introduced the load line to protect his cargoes, and the law was passed quickly because other shipowners strongly supported his campaign from the very beginning."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The Plimsoll line shows the minimum depth a ship must reach before it can sail, and there is only one mark because ships float at exactly the same depth in all kinds of water."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Load lines were introduced in 1876 to reduce insurance costs for shipowners, and they have since been replaced by electronic sensors that measure the weight of the cargo on board directly."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. A campaign against overloading led to the load line, now with several marks. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-22', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Where the date changes', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The International Date Line runs roughly along the one hundred and eightieth meridian, through the Pacific Ocean, and marks where one calendar day ends and the next begins. A traveller crossing it towards the west moves forward a day, while crossing towards the east moves back a day. The need for such a line was noticed centuries ago. When the survivors of Ferdinand Magellan''s expedition returned to Spain in 1522 after sailing around the world westwards, they discovered that their carefully kept log was one day behind the calendar at home. The date line is not straight. It zigzags so that island groups and countries are not divided between two dates. Countries can even choose which side to be on. In 2011, Samoa moved to the other side of the line, skipping the thirtieth of December entirely, so that its working week would match those of its main trading partners, Australia and New Zealand.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The date line follows a perfectly straight path along the one hundred and eightieth meridian, and no country is allowed to change sides, because this would disrupt international trade and travel."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The International Date Line, running roughly through the middle of the Pacific, marks where one day changes to the next. It bends around countries, which can choose their side, as Samoa did in 2011 to match its trading partners."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Magellan''s expedition created the International Date Line in 1522 after its sailors lost a day, and crossing the line towards the east moves a traveller forward to the next day."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Samoa moved to the other side of the date line to become one day behind Australia, which made trade easier, and it repeated the change several times in the following years."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. The line marks the change of date, bends around countries, and Samoa switched sides. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-23', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Finding longitude at sea', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'For centuries, sailors could measure their latitude from the Sun and stars, but longitude, their position east or west, was far harder to determine. Mistakes caused shipwrecks and lost cargoes. One solution was to carry an accurate clock showing the time at the home port: comparing it with local noon at sea revealed how far east or west the ship had travelled. The problem was that ordinary clocks lost accuracy on a moving ship. In 1714, the British government offered a large prize for a practical solution. John Harrison, a largely self-taught clockmaker, spent decades building a series of sea clocks. His fourth design, the size of a large pocket watch, performed remarkably well on a voyage to Jamaica in 1761. Yet the officials responsible, many of whom favoured an astronomical method, were reluctant to pay him in full. Only after years of dispute, and the involvement of the king, did he receive most of the money.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Harrison won the longitude prize immediately in 1714 by inventing a method of measuring latitude from the stars, which officials praised as far more practical than using clocks at sea."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Sailors could already find longitude easily in the eighteenth century, and Harrison''s clocks were designed mainly to measure latitude, a problem that had caused a great many shipwrecks and lost cargoes."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Finding longitude at sea required an accurate clock showing home time. John Harrison spent decades building sea clocks, and his fourth performed very well in 1761, but officials who favoured astronomical methods delayed paying him most of the prize."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Harrison''s first sea clock was small enough to fit in a pocket and worked perfectly, but he refused the prize money because he preferred to keep his clever design secret from others."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. An accurate clock solved longitude, but Harrison struggled to be paid. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-24', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The seven-day cycle', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Unlike the day, the month and the year, the seven-day week is not based directly on any single natural cycle. Its origins are debated, but historians point to several influences. Ancient Babylonians gave special importance to the number seven, and seven celestial bodies could be seen moving across the sky: the Sun, the Moon and five planets. A quarter of a lunar month is also roughly seven days. The Jewish tradition of a weekly day of rest spread the seven-day week further, and it was eventually adopted throughout the Roman world. Many languages still link the days to those celestial bodies: in English, Sunday and Monday refer to the Sun and the Moon, and Saturday to Saturn. The week has proved remarkably resistant to change. Revolutionary France tried a ten-day week, and the Soviet Union experimented with five- and six-day weeks, but both attempts were abandoned.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The seven-day week is based directly on the time the Moon takes to orbit the Earth, and every culture in history has always used exactly the same week without any variation at all."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Revolutionary France and the Soviet Union successfully replaced the seven-day week, and most modern countries now use a ten-day week based on the metric system introduced at that time."}, {"id": "C", "text": "English day names come from Roman emperors rather than from the Sun, Moon and planets, and the seven-day week was invented by the Romans to organise military campaigns across Europe."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The seven-day week has uncertain origins, possibly linked to seven visible celestial bodies, lunar phases and the Jewish day of rest. Many day names still refer to those bodies, and attempts by France and the Soviet Union to change it failed."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Several influences shaped the week, and attempts to change it failed. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-25', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'A number for nothing', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'It is easy to take zero for granted, but for much of history it did not exist as a number. Some ancient civilisations, such as the Babylonians, used a symbol as a placeholder to show an empty position in a number, rather like the gap that distinguishes two hundred and five from twenty-five. However, they did not treat it as a number in its own right. In India, mathematicians went further. In the seventh century, the mathematician Brahmagupta set out rules for calculating with zero, such as the result of adding zero to a number or subtracting a number from itself. Indian numerals, including zero, spread to the Islamic world, where scholars developed them further, and eventually reached Europe. There they were adopted only slowly, but the place-value system they made possible is the foundation of modern mathematics and computing.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Zero was first used by some civilisations only as a placeholder. Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the seventh century, treated it as a number with rules, and the system spread through the Islamic world to Europe, becoming the basis of modern mathematics."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The Babylonians invented zero as a number and set out the rules for calculating with it, and Europeans adopted the system immediately because it was far simpler than their own."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Zero was invented in Europe in the seventh century and later spread to India, where mathematicians used it only as a placeholder rather than as a number in its own right."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Brahmagupta rejected the idea of zero because it caused errors in calculations, so modern computers use a different system that does not depend on the position of digits in a number."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. From placeholder to number in India, then to the Islamic world and Europe. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-26', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Fibonacci''s book of calculation', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In 1202, the Italian mathematician Leonardo of Pisa, now known as Fibonacci, published a book called Liber Abaci. Having grown up partly in North Africa, where his father worked as a trade official, he had learned the numerals used by Arab merchants, which had originated in India. His book showed European readers how these numerals made practical calculations, such as converting currencies and calculating interest, far easier than with Roman numerals. The book also contains a puzzle about breeding rabbits that produces the sequence of numbers now named after him, although the sequence had been described centuries earlier by Indian mathematicians. The new numerals were not accepted everywhere at once. In 1299, the city of Florence banned them in official accounts, partly because they were easier to alter fraudulently, for example by turning a zero into a six or nine.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Fibonacci invented the numerals used today while living in North Africa, and Florence quickly made them compulsory in all official accounts because they were impossible to alter fraudulently in any way."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Fibonacci''s 1202 book introduced Indian-Arabic numerals to Europe, showing how they simplified practical calculations. It includes the sequence named after him, described earlier in India, and adoption was slow, with Florence banning the numerals in official accounts."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Liber Abaci was mainly a book about breeding rabbits, and the number sequence it describes was discovered by Fibonacci for the first time, without any earlier examples from other mathematicians."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Fibonacci argued that Roman numerals were better for converting currencies and calculating interest, and his book persuaded European merchants to keep using them for several more centuries in their trade."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. The book spread new numerals, but acceptance was slow. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-27', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Sixty minutes in an hour', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Why are there sixty minutes in an hour and sixty seconds in a minute, when most of our counting is based on tens? The answer goes back to ancient Mesopotamia, where the Babylonians used a number system based on sixty. Sixty is a convenient number because it can be divided evenly in many ways: by two, three, four, five, six, ten, twelve, fifteen, twenty and thirty. This made fractions much easier to handle, especially in astronomy, and it also explains the three hundred and sixty degrees in a circle. The division of the day into twenty-four hours is usually traced to ancient Egypt, where daytime and night were each divided into twelve parts. Attempts to change these units have failed. During the French Revolution, clocks showing ten hours a day, each of one hundred minutes, were introduced, but the system was abandoned after only a couple of years.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The Babylonians chose sixty because it is a multiple of ten, and the twenty-four-hour day was introduced during the French Revolution, replacing an older ten-hour system used across Europe."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The number sixty was chosen because it is difficult to divide, which prevented errors in astronomy, and decimal clocks are still widely used in France for many official purposes today."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Our minutes and seconds come from the Babylonian number system based on sixty, which divides evenly in many ways and also gave us 360 degrees. The twenty-four-hour day comes from Egypt, and France''s attempt at decimal time was quickly abandoned."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Ancient Egyptians divided the hour into sixty minutes, while the Babylonians invented the twenty-four-hour day, and both systems were later replaced by decimal time in most of Europe for centuries."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Base sixty from Babylon, twenty-four hours from Egypt, decimal time abandoned. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-28', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Prime numbers and online security', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Every time you buy something online, your payment details are protected by encryption, and some of the most widely used methods rely on prime numbers. Multiplying two large prime numbers together is easy for a computer, even if each has hundreds of digits. But reversing the process, taking the result and working out which two primes produced it, is extraordinarily difficult. With current computers, it could take an impractically long time. A system published in 1977, known as RSA after the initials of its inventors, uses this difference. It allows a website to publish a public key that anyone can use to lock a message, while only the owner of a matching private key can unlock it. Researchers are concerned that future quantum computers could solve the factoring problem much faster, which is why new encryption methods designed to resist them are already being developed and introduced.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Encryption works because dividing large numbers by primes is impossible for computers, and quantum computers are expected to make current methods even more secure in the future than they are today."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The RSA system uses a single secret key that is shared by the website and its customers, and it relies on the fact that multiplying prime numbers takes computers a very long time."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Prime numbers are no longer used in online payments because researchers found a quick way to factor large numbers in 1977, which led directly to the development of quantum computers."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Some encryption relies on the fact that multiplying large primes is easy but finding the original primes from the result is extremely hard. RSA uses this for public and private keys, but quantum computers could threaten it, so new methods are being developed."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Easy multiplication and hard factoring underpin RSA, which quantum computers may threaten. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-29', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Three doors and a car', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Imagine a game show with three closed doors. Behind one is a car; behind the other two are goats. You choose a door. The host, who knows what is behind each door, then opens one of the doors you did not choose, always revealing a goat, and asks whether you want to switch to the remaining closed door. Most people feel it makes no difference: two doors are left, so the chance must be fifty-fifty. In fact, switching doubles your chance of winning, from one in three to two in three. Your first choice had only a one-in-three chance of being right, and the host''s action does not change that; if you were wrong, the car must be behind the other door. When a magazine columnist explained this in 1990, she received thousands of letters insisting she was mistaken, including some from people with doctorates. Computer simulations have since confirmed the answer many times.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "In the Monty Hall problem, a contestant who switches doors after the host reveals a goat wins two times in three, not half the time. Many readers, including highly educated ones, rejected this answer in 1990, but simulations have repeatedly confirmed it."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Once the host opens a door, the chance of winning is exactly fifty-fifty, so switching makes no difference, as a magazine columnist correctly explained to her many readers in her column in 1990."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Switching doors in the game reduces the chance of winning the car, because the host deliberately opens the door hiding the prize, which misleads most contestants into changing their original choice."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The Monty Hall problem shows that contestants who stay with their first choice win two times in three, and computer simulations have shown that mathematicians were right to criticise the columnist."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. Switching wins two times in three, despite widespread disbelief. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-30', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Shared birthdays', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'How many people need to be in a room before there is a better than even chance that two of them share a birthday? Many people guess around one hundred and eighty, half the number of days in a year. The real answer is just twenty-three. With seventy people, a shared birthday is almost certain. The result seems surprising because we tend to think about the chance that someone shares our own birthday, which is low. But the question is about any two people in the room. Among twenty-three people, there are two hundred and fifty-three possible pairs, and each pair is a chance for a match. The same mathematics matters in computer security. Systems that produce short digital fingerprints for files can be attacked by searching for two different files with the same fingerprint, which is much easier than matching one particular file, a technique known as a birthday attack.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "At least one hundred and eighty people are needed before two are likely to share a birthday, and computer systems use this fact to make digital fingerprints impossible for attackers to copy."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Only twenty-three people are needed for an even chance that two share a birthday, because the question concerns any pair, and there are many pairs. The same principle explains birthday attacks, which search for any two files with matching digital fingerprints."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The birthday paradox shows that the chance of someone sharing your own birthday is very high in a group of twenty-three, which is why birthday attacks are aimed at individual people."}, {"id": "D", "text": "With seventy people in a room there is only a small chance of a shared birthday, and the paradox has no practical use outside mathematics classrooms and books of puzzles."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Many possible pairs make a match likely; the idea applies to computer security. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-31', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The first-digit pattern', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'If you look at the first digits of numbers in many real-world collections of data, such as populations of towns, lengths of rivers or company accounts, you might expect each digit from one to nine to appear equally often. Surprisingly, it does not. The digit one appears first about thirty percent of the time, while nine appears first less than five percent of the time. This pattern is known as Benford''s law. It was first noticed in the nineteenth century by an astronomer who observed that the early pages of books of logarithm tables, used for numbers beginning with one, were more worn than later pages. The law has a practical use. When people invent numbers, they tend to spread the first digits too evenly, so auditors sometimes check whether financial figures follow the expected pattern. A mismatch does not prove fraud, but it can suggest where to look more closely.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Benford''s law states that each digit from one to nine appears first equally often in real data, so auditors look carefully for numbers that begin too often with the digit one."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The pattern was discovered by an auditor who noticed that fraudsters always choose the digit nine, and any mismatch with Benford''s law is now accepted as legal proof of fraud."}, {"id": "C", "text": "In many real data sets, numbers begin with the digit one far more often than with nine, a pattern called Benford''s law. Because invented figures rarely follow it, auditors use it to find accounts that deserve closer investigation, though not as proof of fraud."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Benford''s law applies only to numbers invented by people, which is why logarithm tables showed more wear on their later pages, and it has no use in checking financial figures."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Low first digits dominate real data, which helps auditors spot invented figures. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-32', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The missing bullet holes', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'During the Second World War, analysts studied bombers returning from missions and recorded where they had been hit. Bullet holes were concentrated on the wings and the body of the aircraft, while the engines showed relatively few. An obvious conclusion was to add armour where the holes were most common. The statistician Abraham Wald pointed out a flaw in this reasoning. The analysts were only looking at planes that had survived. Aircraft hit in the engines were less likely to return at all, so the lack of holes there did not mean the engines were rarely hit; it meant that hits there were deadly. The armour, he argued, should go where the returning planes had no damage. This mistake is called survivorship bias, and it appears in many areas. For example, studying only successful companies can make risky strategies look safer than they really are, because the failures are no longer visible.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Abraham Wald recommended adding armour to the wings and body of bombers, where most bullet holes were found, because these areas were clearly the most vulnerable to enemy fire during missions."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Survivorship bias means that successful companies always use safer strategies than failed ones, and Wald showed this by studying aircraft engines that had been repaired after the war in military factories."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The engines of returning bombers were covered in bullet holes, so analysts concluded that engines needed extra armour, a decision that Wald said was based on complete data from every mission."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Analysts wanted to armour bombers where returning planes had most bullet holes, but Abraham Wald noted that planes hit elsewhere, such as the engines, had not returned. This error, survivorship bias, also affects conclusions drawn from studying only successful companies."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Only survivors were studied, so the missing damage pointed to the real weak spots. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-33', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Ice cream and drowning', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In many places, ice cream sales and the number of drownings rise and fall together throughout the year. Does eating ice cream cause people to drown? Of course not. Both increase in hot weather, when more people buy ice cream and more people go swimming. This is a classic example of the difference between correlation and causation. Two things can move together because a third factor, sometimes called a confounding variable, affects both of them. Sometimes the link is pure coincidence; sometimes the direction of cause is reversed. For this reason, scientists prefer randomised experiments, in which people are assigned to different groups by chance, so that other factors are spread evenly between them. When experiments are impossible or unethical, researchers must use observational data with great care, trying to account for possible confounding factors before drawing conclusions about cause.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Ice cream sales and drownings rise together because hot weather affects both, showing that correlation does not prove causation. Randomised experiments help rule out such hidden factors, and when they are impossible, observational data must be interpreted with great care."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Research has shown that eating ice cream increases the risk of drowning, which is why scientists recommend observational studies rather than randomised experiments when studying the causes of accidents at the beach."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Correlations between two things are always coincidences, so scientists ignore them completely and rely only on experiments that assign people to groups according to their own personal preferences and daily habits."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Hot weather reduces both ice cream sales and swimming, and the speaker argues that confounding variables are rare, so observational data can usually be trusted without special care or checking."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. A third factor explains the link, which is why randomised experiments are preferred. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-34', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Regression to the mean', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'When a result is unusually good or unusually bad, the next result is likely to be closer to average, simply because extreme results usually involve some luck. This is called regression to the mean, and it can easily lead to false conclusions. The psychologist Daniel Kahneman described flight instructors who believed that praising a trainee after an excellent landing made the next landing worse, while criticising a poor landing made the next one better. In fact, the changes would have happened anyway: after an exceptional performance, an average one is more likely to follow. The same effect can make policies look more successful than they are. Speed cameras, for example, are often installed at locations after an unusually high number of accidents. If accidents then fall, part of the fall may be due to regression to the mean rather than the cameras, so careful studies compare these sites with similar ones.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Kahneman showed that criticising trainee pilots improves their landings while praise makes them worse, which proves that flight instructors should never encourage their students, even after very good performances in training."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Extreme results are usually followed by more average ones because luck plays a part, a pattern called regression to the mean. It misled flight instructors about criticism and can exaggerate the success of measures such as speed cameras installed after accident spikes."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Regression to the mean means that unusually good results are usually followed by even better ones, so speed cameras always cause large falls in accidents on the roads where they are installed."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Speed cameras are placed randomly across road networks to avoid regression to the mean, and studies comparing sites show that luck has no effect whatsoever on accident numbers over time."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Luck makes extremes fade, which can mislead judgements about praise and policies. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-35', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'The horse that could count', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In the early twentieth century, a horse in Germany called Clever Hans appeared to solve arithmetic problems, tapping his hoof to give the answer. Investigators eventually discovered that the horse was responding to tiny, unconscious changes in the posture and facial expressions of the people around him, who knew the answer. The case illustrates why modern medical trials are often double-blind. In such a trial, neither the participants nor the researchers who assess them know who is receiving the real treatment and who is receiving a placebo or a comparison treatment. If participants know, their expectations can affect how they feel and what they report. If researchers know, they may unintentionally treat patients differently or interpret results more favourably. By keeping everyone unaware until the results are analysed, double-blind trials help ensure that any difference between the groups is caused by the treatment itself.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Clever Hans could genuinely solve arithmetic problems, and his case showed that animals should be used in medical trials instead of human participants, whose expectations affect results in many ways."}, {"id": "B", "text": "In a double-blind trial, participants choose whether to receive the real treatment or a placebo, while researchers know exactly which patients are in each group from the start of the study."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The story of a horse responding to people''s unconscious cues shows how expectations affect results. In double-blind trials neither participants nor assessing researchers know who receives the real treatment, helping ensure differences are caused by the treatment itself."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Double-blind trials were introduced because researchers deliberately changed results, and the speaker argues that participants'' expectations have no effect at all on how they feel or what they report."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Unconscious cues explain Clever Hans; blinding stops expectations shaping results. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-36', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Repeating psychology studies', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In 2015, a large group of researchers published the results of an ambitious project. They had tried to repeat one hundred studies published in leading psychology journals, following the original methods as closely as possible. Only around a third to two fifths of the repeated studies produced clearly similar results. The project drew attention to what became known as the replication crisis. Researchers identified several contributing factors. Many original studies used small samples, which make chance findings more likely. Journals tended to publish surprising positive results rather than studies that found nothing, and researchers sometimes tried many different ways of analysing their data until one produced an interesting result. The problem is not limited to psychology. In response, many scientists now register their plans before collecting data, use larger samples and share their data, so that others can check their work more easily.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "A 2015 project found that nearly all psychology studies could be repeated successfully, which showed that concerns about small samples and publication practices had been greatly exaggerated by earlier critics."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The replication crisis affects only psychology, and it was caused by researchers sharing their data too freely, so scientists now keep their data private until the results are published in journals."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Journals have always preferred to publish studies that found nothing, and the speaker argues that larger samples make chance findings more likely, so smaller studies are now recommended by experts."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A 2015 project repeated one hundred psychology studies and found that only a minority produced similar results. Small samples, a preference for surprising findings and flexible analysis contributed, and researchers now register plans, use larger samples and share data."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Few studies replicated; causes were identified and practices have changed. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-37', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Alfred Nobel''s fortune', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Alfred Nobel, the Swedish chemist who invented dynamite, made a fortune from explosives. According to a well-known story, in 1888 a French newspaper mistakenly published his obituary when it was actually his brother who had died, and described him as a merchant of death who had grown rich by finding ways to kill people faster. Historians are not certain how much this episode influenced him, but in his will, signed in 1895, Nobel left most of his fortune to establish prizes for those who had conferred the greatest benefit on humankind in physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and peace. The first prizes were awarded in 1901. A prize in economics was added much later, in 1968, funded by Sweden''s central bank in Nobel''s memory, which is why it is officially not one of the original Nobel Prizes.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Alfred Nobel made his fortune from explosives, and a mistaken obituary calling him a merchant of death may have influenced his decision to fund prizes in his will. The first were awarded in 1901, while the economics prize was added in 1968."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Alfred Nobel founded the prizes after he was awarded one for inventing dynamite, and the economics prize was among the original categories named in his will in 1895 by his family."}, {"id": "C", "text": "A French newspaper published Nobel''s obituary after his death in 1888, and his brother used the family fortune to create prizes, which historians say had nothing to do with explosives."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Historians are certain that the mistaken obituary was the only reason Nobel created the prizes, which were first awarded in 1895 in physics, chemistry and economics, among several other fields."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. The obituary story may have influenced the will; economics was added much later. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-38', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Linnaeus''s binomial names', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Before the eighteenth century, naturalists often identified plants and animals using long descriptive phrases in Latin, sometimes a dozen words long. The Swedish botanist Carl Linnaeus popularised a much simpler approach in the 1750s. He gave each species a two-part name: the first word identifies the group it belongs to, called the genus, and the second identifies the particular species. Humans, for example, are Homo sapiens. Latin was used because it was the shared language of scholars across Europe, and because, as a language no longer changing in everyday speech, its meanings were stable. The system solves a practical problem. The same animal may have many different common names in different languages and regions, while one common name may refer to several different species. A scientific name, by contrast, refers to one species only, allowing researchers anywhere in the world to be sure they are discussing the same organism.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Linnaeus introduced long descriptive phrases to name species, replacing the two-part names used earlier, because common names were more precise than Latin in most languages of Europe at that time."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Linnaeus replaced long Latin descriptions with two-part names for each species, showing its genus and species. Latin was a stable, shared scholarly language, and scientific names avoid the confusion caused by varied common names across languages and regions."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Scientific names are written in Latin because it was the everyday language of Sweden in the eighteenth century, and each scientific name may refer to several different species in different regions."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The two-part naming system was abandoned in the twentieth century because researchers in different countries preferred local common names that everyone could easily understand without any special scientific training."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Two-part Latin names give each species one unambiguous name. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-39', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Gaps in the periodic table', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'In 1869, the Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev published a table arranging the known chemical elements in order of their atomic weight, grouping together elements with similar properties. Others had noticed patterns before, but Mendeleev took a bold step. Where the pattern suggested an element should exist but none was known, he left a gap, and he predicted the properties the missing elements would have, including their approximate weight and density. He also changed the order of some elements when their properties did not fit, suggesting that their measured weights might be wrong. Within about two decades, several of the predicted elements were discovered, including gallium and germanium, with properties remarkably close to his forecasts. These successful predictions did more than anything else to convince scientists that his table reflected a real underlying order in nature.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Mendeleev was the first person to notice any pattern among the elements, and his table was accepted immediately because it included every element that exists today in the natural world."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Mendeleev refused to change the order of any elements, even when their properties did not fit, and his predictions about the missing elements turned out to be inaccurate in most cases."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Mendeleev arranged the elements by weight and properties, leaving gaps for undiscovered elements and predicting their properties. When elements such as gallium and germanium were found with properties close to his predictions, scientists were convinced his table reflected a real order."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Gallium and germanium were already known in 1869, and Mendeleev used them to test his table, which scientists around the world accepted only after his death, many decades later, in Russia."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. The gaps and predictions, later confirmed, convinced scientists. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-40', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'A new definition of the kilogram', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'For more than a century, the kilogram was defined by a single physical object: a small cylinder of platinum and iridium kept in a secure vault near Paris since 1889. Every other measurement of mass in the world was ultimately traced back to it. This created a problem. When the cylinder was compared with its official copies over the decades, their masses were found to have drifted apart by tiny amounts, a few hundredths of a milligram. Since the cylinder was the kilogram by definition, nobody could say for certain whether it had lost mass or the copies had gained it. In 2019, scientists redefined the kilogram using a fundamental constant of nature, called the Planck constant, which can be measured with extremely precise instruments. The cylinder still exists, but it is no longer the standard, and every basic unit of measurement is now defined by constants rather than by objects.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The kilogram cylinder near Paris was found to have lost exactly one gram, so it was destroyed in 2019 and replaced by a new cylinder made from a more stable metal."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Scientists redefined the kilogram because the Paris vault was no longer secure, and the new definition uses copies of the cylinder kept in many laboratories around the world for comparison."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The kilogram is now the only unit still defined by a physical object, because constants of nature cannot yet be measured precisely enough to replace the cylinder kept near Paris."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The kilogram used to be defined by a metal cylinder near Paris, but comparisons showed it and its copies drifting slightly apart. In 2019 it was redefined using a constant of nature, so all basic units now rest on constants rather than objects."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Drift between cylinders led to a definition based on a constant. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-41', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Counting between flash and thunder', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'During a thunderstorm, we see the flash of lightning before we hear the thunder, even though both are produced at the same moment. The reason is the enormous difference in speed between light and sound. Light travels so fast that it reaches us almost instantly, while sound travels through the air at roughly three hundred and forty metres per second. This allows a simple estimate of distance: count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, and divide by three to get the approximate distance in kilometres. Thunder itself is caused by the lightning heating the air around it to extremely high temperatures in a fraction of a second, making it expand violently. Thunder can usually be heard up to about fifteen kilometres away, and safety experts point out that if you can hear it, you are close enough to be struck, so you should move indoors.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "We see lightning before hearing thunder because light travels far faster than sound, so counting the seconds and dividing by three estimates the distance in kilometres. Thunder comes from rapidly heated air expanding, and if it can be heard, people should shelter indoors."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Lightning and thunder are produced at different moments, which is why we see the flash first, and thunder can be heard safely from any distance, so there is no need to shelter."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Sound travels faster than light, so thunder should arrive first, but the flash of lightning is brighter, and we notice it earlier because our eyes react more quickly than our ears."}, {"id": "D", "text": "To estimate the distance of a storm, count the seconds between flash and thunder and multiply by three, and thunder is caused by clouds colliding high in the atmosphere during storms."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. The speed difference gives a distance estimate; hearing thunder means danger. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-42', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Astronomical distances', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Although a light-year sounds like a measure of time, it is actually a measure of distance: the distance light travels in one year, which is roughly nine and a half trillion kilometres. Astronomers use it because distances in space are so vast that kilometres become impractical. The nearest star to the Sun, Proxima Centauri, is just over four light-years away, meaning its light takes more than four years to reach us. This has an interesting consequence. When we look at distant objects, we see them not as they are now but as they were when the light left them. The Andromeda Galaxy, the most distant object easily visible to the naked eye, appears as it was around two and a half million years ago. Powerful telescopes can observe galaxies whose light set out billions of years ago, allowing astronomers to study the early history of the universe.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "A light-year is a unit of time used by astronomers to measure how long stars live, and the nearest star to the Sun is about four million light-years away from the Earth."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A light-year is the distance light travels in a year, used because distances in space are so large. Since light takes time to arrive, we see distant objects as they were in the past, which lets telescopes study the early universe."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Light from the Andromeda Galaxy reaches us instantly, so we see it exactly as it is today, while only very powerful telescopes can show how nearby stars looked in the past."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Astronomers prefer kilometres to light-years because they are more precise, and looking at distant galaxies tells us about the future of the universe rather than about its early history."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. A distance unit, and looking far means looking back in time. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-43', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Chlorophyll''s colour', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Plants are green because of chlorophyll, the pigment they use to capture energy from sunlight for photosynthesis. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light strongly, but it absorbs much less green light, which is reflected or passes through the leaf. That reflected green light is what our eyes detect. At first this seems puzzling. Sunlight contains a great deal of green light, so why would plants waste it? Scientists have proposed several explanations. One recent idea is that absorbing light in two separate colour bands helps plants keep a steady supply of energy when light conditions change rapidly, for example when clouds pass over or leaves move in the wind, protecting them from sudden surges that could cause damage. Leaves also contain other pigments, such as yellow and orange ones, which are usually hidden by the chlorophyll and become visible when it breaks down.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Chlorophyll absorbs green light more strongly than any other colour, which is why leaves look green, and scientists agree that this helps plants store extra energy for the winter months."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Plants are green because sunlight contains very little green light, so they have no reason to absorb it, and the yellow pigments in leaves are produced only in the autumn each year."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Plants look green because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light but reflects green. Why they use less green light is still debated; one idea is that absorbing two colour bands keeps energy steady in changing light. Other hidden pigments appear when chlorophyll breaks down."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Leaves contain only one pigment, chlorophyll, and they turn yellow when it absorbs too much red and blue light during long periods of cloudy or windy weather in the summer."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Green is reflected; the reason plants use less of it is debated. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-44', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Pink flamingos', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Flamingos are not born pink. Chicks hatch with grey or white feathers, and adults only become pink because of what they eat. Their diet of algae and tiny shrimp-like animals contains pigments called carotenoids, the same family of pigments that makes carrots orange. The birds'' bodies break these pigments down and deposit them in their feathers, skin and legs. A flamingo that stops eating enough of these foods will gradually become paler. For this reason, zoos add carotenoids to the diet of captive flamingos to keep their colour. The colour also appears to matter in social life. Brighter birds tend to be healthier and more successful at attracting a partner, and researchers have observed that flamingos spread coloured oils from a gland near their tail onto their feathers, particularly during the breeding season, making themselves look pinker, rather like applying make-up.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Flamingo chicks are born bright pink and gradually become paler as adults, which is why zoos add special oils to their feathers to restore their colour before the breeding season."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Flamingos produce their pink colour themselves, whatever they eat, and the colour has no effect on their health or on their success at finding a partner during the breeding season."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The pink colour comes from carrots, which flamingos eat in large amounts in the wild, and zoos have found that feeding them shrimp makes them turn grey within a few weeks."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Flamingos become pink from carotenoid pigments in their diet, not at birth, and fade without them, so zoos supplement their food. Colour matters socially, as brighter birds attract partners, and flamingos even spread coloured oils on their feathers when breeding."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. Diet provides the colour, which also matters for attracting partners. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-45', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'A snake''s forked tongue', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'When a snake flicks its tongue, it is not tasting the air in the usual sense, and it is certainly not preparing to sting. It is collecting chemical particles from the air and the ground. The tongue carries these particles back into the mouth, where they are passed to a special sensory organ in the roof of the mouth, sometimes called the Jacobson''s organ, which analyses them. The forked shape of the tongue is particularly useful. Because the two tips sample the air at slightly different points, the snake can compare the strength of a scent on its left and right sides, and turn towards the stronger one. This allows it to follow the trail of prey, or of a potential mate, with considerable accuracy. Many lizards use a similar system, and species that rely heavily on following scent trails tend to have more deeply forked tongues.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Snakes flick their tongues to collect chemical particles, which a special organ in the roof of the mouth analyses. The forked tongue lets them compare scent on each side and follow trails of prey or mates, a system many lizards share."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Snakes use their tongues mainly to taste food before swallowing it, and the forked shape has no particular purpose, since both tips collect exactly the same information from the air."}, {"id": "C", "text": "A snake flicks its tongue as a warning before it stings, and the particles it collects are analysed in its nose, which is why snakes have such a strong sense of smell."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The Jacobson''s organ is found in the snake''s forked tongue, and lizards cannot follow scent trails because their tongues are not forked like those of snakes in any way at all."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. The tongue gathers scent for an organ in the mouth, and the fork gives direction. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-46', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Following the trail', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'When a foraging ant finds food, it returns to the nest laying down a chemical trail called a pheromone. Other ants follow the trail to the food and, as they return, add more pheromone, strengthening it. This simple system allows colonies to find efficient routes without any ant understanding the overall picture. In a classic experiment, ants were given two bridges of different lengths leading to the same food. At first they used both, but the ants on the shorter bridge completed their round trips faster, so pheromone built up more quickly on that route, and soon most ants were using it. Because pheromones gradually evaporate, trails to exhausted food sources fade away. Computer scientists have been inspired by this behaviour. Programs based on virtual ants laying virtual trails have been used to find efficient routes in problems such as planning deliveries and directing data through networks.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "A leader ant plans the shortest route to food and instructs the others, and computer scientists have copied this by designing programs that are controlled by a single central computer at all times."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Ants mark routes to food with pheromone trails that others follow and reinforce. Shorter routes gain stronger trails because trips are faster, while unused trails fade. This behaviour has inspired computer programs that find efficient routes for deliveries and networks."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Pheromone trails never fade, so ants continue visiting food sources long after the food is gone, which is why the longer bridge in the experiment was used most often by the ants."}, {"id": "D", "text": "In the bridge experiment, ants avoided both routes because the pheromone was too strong, and computer scientists concluded that ant behaviour has no practical use in planning deliveries or networks."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. Reinforced trails find short routes without a leader, inspiring route-finding programs. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-47', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'A brainless problem solver', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The slime mould Physarum polycephalum is a single-celled organism with no brain or nervous system, yet it can solve problems that seem to require intelligence. It spreads out as a yellow network of tubes, searching for food. When it finds food, the tubes leading to it thicken, while tubes that lead nowhere shrink and disappear. In 2000, Japanese researchers showed that a slime mould placed in a maze with food at two exits would eventually form a single tube along the shortest route. Ten years later, researchers placed pieces of food on a surface in the pattern of the cities around Tokyo. The network the slime mould formed was remarkably similar to the region''s actual railway network in its efficiency and resilience. The researchers suggested that studying such simple organisms could help engineers design transport and communication networks that adapt without central planning.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Slime moulds have a simple brain that allows them to learn routes through mazes, and researchers used them to plan the original railway network around the city of Tokyo in the 1960s."}, {"id": "B", "text": "When a slime mould finds food, its tubes shrink and disappear, so it cannot find the shortest route, and researchers concluded that it is of no use to engineers designing networks."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Although it has no brain, a slime mould strengthens tubes leading to food and abandons others, allowing it to find the shortest route through a maze. It even formed a network resembling Tokyo''s railway, which may help engineers design adaptable networks."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The Tokyo experiment showed that slime moulds form networks that are far less efficient than real railways, proving that central planning is always necessary when designing transport and communication systems."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is the correct summary. Simple tube growth finds efficient networks, which interests engineers. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-48', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Turritopsis', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'A small jellyfish known as Turritopsis dohrnii, only a few millimetres across, has earned the nickname the immortal jellyfish. Most jellyfish begin life as a polyp attached to the sea floor, grow into a free-swimming adult, reproduce and die. When this species is injured, stressed or ageing, however, it can do something extraordinary. Instead of dying, the adult sinks, transforms back into a polyp, and begins its life cycle again. Its cells change from one type into another, a process scientists are studying closely. In theory, the cycle could repeat many times. In practice, most individuals in the wild are eaten by predators or killed by disease long before this matters, so the jellyfish is not truly immortal. Researchers hope that understanding how its cells reverse their development could contribute to research on ageing and tissue repair.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The immortal jellyfish cannot die from any cause, including predators and disease, and scientists have already used it to develop new treatments that reverse ageing in humans in recent years."}, {"id": "B", "text": "This jellyfish begins life as a free-swimming adult and later settles on the sea floor as a polyp, where it lives for thousands of years without changing its form at all."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Turritopsis dohrnii is the largest jellyfish in the ocean, and it becomes immortal only in laboratories, where its cells are artificially changed from one type to another by teams of scientists."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The jellyfish Turritopsis dohrnii can turn from an adult back into a polyp when injured or ageing, potentially repeating its life cycle. Predators and disease kill most individuals, but its changing cells interest researchers studying ageing and tissue repair."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is the correct summary. It can reverse its life cycle but is not truly immortal in the wild. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-49', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Eighteen holes', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'Why does a standard round of golf have eighteen holes? Early golf courses in Scotland had no fixed number: some had five, some seven, some twelve or more, depending on the land available. The Old Course at St Andrews, one of the most influential, originally had twenty-two holes, played out along the coast and back again. In 1764, the golfers there decided that the first four holes were too short and combined them into two, which reduced each half of the course by two holes and left a round of eighteen. Because St Andrews became the leading authority on the rules of golf, other clubs gradually followed its example, and eighteen became the standard. A popular story claims that the number was chosen because a bottle of whisky contained eighteen shots, but historians regard this as a legend with no evidence behind it.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Early Scottish golf courses had varying numbers of holes. St Andrews reduced its twenty-two holes to eighteen in 1764 by combining short ones, and because it became the leading authority on golf''s rules, other clubs followed. The whisky story is a legend."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Golf has eighteen holes because a bottle of whisky contained eighteen shots, a fact confirmed by historians who found records of the decision at St Andrews in 1764 among old club papers."}, {"id": "C", "text": "St Andrews originally had twelve holes and added six more in 1764 to make the game longer, but most other clubs refused to follow its example for many years afterwards."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The number of holes was fixed at eighteen when golf was invented, and every early course in Scotland had exactly the same layout, regardless of the land that was available to it."}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is the correct summary. St Andrews'' 1764 change became the standard; the whisky story is a legend. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hcs-50', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'An ancient vending machine', 'You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.', NULL, 'The vending machine may seem like a modern invention, but the earliest known design is nearly two thousand years old. In the first century, the engineer Hero of Alexandria described a device for dispensing holy water in temples. When a worshipper dropped a coin through a slot, it landed on a small pan attached to a lever. The weight of the coin tipped the lever, which opened a valve and released a measured amount of water. When the coin slid off the pan, the lever returned to its original position and the flow stopped. Modern coin-operated machines appeared in the 1880s, when machines selling postcards were installed in London. Today vending machines sell everything from drinks to hot meals, and Japan is famous for having one of the highest numbers of machines per person in the world, partly because of low crime rates, which make it safe to leave them outdoors.', 10, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Hero of Alexandria''s machine used electricity to release holy water, and vending machines then disappeared until the twentieth century, when they were first introduced in Japan to sell drinks and food."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The first known vending machine, described by Hero of Alexandria, used a coin''s weight to tip a lever and release holy water. Modern machines began in 1880s London, and Japan now has very many, partly because low crime makes outdoor machines safe."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The first vending machines sold postcards in ancient Alexandria, and Japan has very few machines today because high crime rates make it unsafe to leave them outdoors in public places."}, {"id": "D", "text": "In Hero''s design, the coin stayed on the pan permanently, so the water flowed until the temple staff turned it off, and modern machines still use exactly the same method today."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is the correct summary. An ancient coin-and-lever design; modern machines from the 1880s; many in Japan. The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Correct Summary']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The first kidney transplant', 'Why was the 1954 transplant successful?', NULL, 'For centuries, doctors dreamed of replacing failing organs, but early attempts at transplants almost always failed. The main obstacle was the immune system, which recognises tissue from another person as foreign and attacks it, a process called rejection. In 1954, surgeons in Boston carried out the first successful long-term kidney transplant. The patient''s donor was his identical twin brother. Because identical twins have almost perfectly matching tissues, the patient''s immune system did not reject the new kidney, and he lived for another eight years. Transplants between people who were not identical twins remained very risky until drugs were developed to suppress the immune system. One of the most important of these was introduced in the early 1980s and greatly improved survival. Today, kidney transplants are routine operations in many countries, and the main limit is no longer surgical skill but the shortage of donated organs.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The donor and the patient were identical twins"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The patient was given drugs to suppress his immune system"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The kidney came from an animal donor"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The surgeons had removed the patient''s immune system"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Matching tissue between identical twins prevented rejection; the drugs came decades later.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Heating wine and milk', 'According to the speaker, where did pasteurisation have its greatest impact?', NULL, 'In the 1860s, French winemakers asked the chemist Louis Pasteur why so much of their wine was turning sour. Pasteur examined samples under a microscope and found that spoilage was caused by unwanted microorganisms. He showed that heating wine gently, to a temperature well below boiling, for a short time killed most of these microbes without ruining the taste. The process became known as pasteurisation. Although it was first developed for wine and beer, its greatest impact came later, when it was applied to milk. In the nineteenth century, milk was a common source of dangerous infections, including tuberculosis, especially in growing cities. Laws requiring milk to be pasteurised helped to reduce these diseases dramatically. Today the same principle, using carefully controlled heat to make food safer while keeping its quality, is applied to juices, eggs and many other products.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "In improving the flavour of wine"}, {"id": "B", "text": "In making milk safer to drink"}, {"id": "C", "text": "In curing patients with tuberculosis"}, {"id": "D", "text": "In extending the life of fresh eggs"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The speaker says its greatest impact came when it was applied to milk.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Clarence Birdseye', 'Why did the fish Birdseye saw in Labrador still taste fresh?', NULL, 'In the 1910s, the American naturalist Clarence Birdseye spent several winters in Labrador, in northern Canada. There he watched Inuit fishers catch fish in temperatures of around minus forty degrees. The fish froze almost instantly in the icy air, and when they were cooked months later they still tasted fresh. Birdseye realised why. When food freezes slowly, large ice crystals form and damage its cells, so it becomes soft and watery when thawed. Rapid freezing produces much smaller crystals, which cause far less damage. Back in the United States, he developed machines that froze packaged food quickly between cold metal plates. His business was sold in 1929 for a very large sum, and the brand still carries his name. However, frozen food only became common in homes decades later, once household freezers and supermarket freezer cabinets were widespread.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The fishers preserved them with salt before freezing"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They were stored between cold metal plates"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They froze so quickly that only small ice crystals formed"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They were cooked immediately after being caught"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Rapid freezing produced small crystals that did little damage to the cells.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-04', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The story of ketchup', 'Why did the Heinz company add more vinegar and sugar?', NULL, 'The word ketchup probably comes from the name of a fermented fish sauce made in southern China and parts of Southeast Asia. British sailors and traders encountered it in the seventeenth century, and English cooks tried to recreate it at home, using ingredients such as mushrooms, walnuts and oysters. Tomato ketchup appeared in the United States in the early nineteenth century, but early versions often spoiled quickly, and some manufacturers added questionable chemicals to preserve them. In the early twentieth century, the company founded by Henry Heinz developed a recipe with much more vinegar and sugar, which preserved the sauce naturally without such additives. It also sold ketchup in clear glass bottles so that customers could see the product was pure. That sweeter, thicker recipe became the model for the ketchup now eaten around the world, far removed from its salty, fishy origins.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To make it taste like the original fish sauce"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To reduce the cost of glass bottles"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To replace the tomatoes with mushrooms"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To preserve the sauce without questionable additives"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Vinegar and sugar preserved the sauce naturally, replacing doubtful chemicals.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-05', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The price of saffron', 'According to the speaker, why is saffron so expensive?', NULL, 'Saffron is often described as the most expensive spice in the world by weight, and the reason lies in how it is harvested. It comes from a crocus flower, and each flower produces only three thin red threads, called stigmas. These must be picked by hand, usually early in the morning, because the flowers open for only a short time in autumn. Estimates suggest that well over one hundred thousand flowers are needed to produce a single kilogram of dried saffron. Machines have so far been unable to replace skilled workers, because the threads are delicate. Iran produces the great majority of the world''s supply, although saffron is also grown in Spain, India and elsewhere. Because of its high price, saffron is also a common target for fraud, with some sellers mixing in dyed plant fibres or other parts of the flower.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Its threads must be picked by hand from huge numbers of flowers"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It can only be grown in one country"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Most of each harvest is lost to fraud"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Its flowers open only once every few years"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Each flower gives only three threads, and all must be picked by hand.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-06', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Decaffeination', 'Which statement about modern decaffeinated coffee is correct?', NULL, 'Decaffeinated coffee has a surprising history. In the early twentieth century, the German merchant Ludwig Roselius is said to have received a shipment of coffee beans that had been soaked in seawater during the voyage. The beans had lost much of their caffeine but still tasted of coffee. Roselius developed a commercial process, but it used benzene, a chemical later found to be harmful, and it was eventually abandoned. Today caffeine is removed from green beans before they are roasted. Some methods use solvents that bind to caffeine; others use only water, passing it through filters that capture caffeine molecules; and some use carbon dioxide under very high pressure. None of these methods removes caffeine completely. Decaffeinated coffee usually keeps a small amount, which is why people who must avoid caffeine entirely are advised to check carefully before drinking it.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It is made from beans after they have been roasted"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It still contains a small amount of caffeine"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It is usually processed with benzene"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It must be soaked in seawater during transport"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The speaker says no method removes caffeine completely.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-07', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Ripening bananas', 'Why are bananas shipped in cool conditions?', NULL, 'Many fruits, including bananas, tomatoes and avocados, continue to ripen after they are picked. The trigger is ethylene, a simple gas that the fruit produces itself. As ethylene levels rise, starches turn into sugars, the fruit softens and its colour changes. This explains the old saying that one bad apple spoils the barrel: a damaged or overripe fruit releases ethylene, which speeds up ripening in the fruit around it. Commercial growers make use of this. Bananas are usually harvested while still hard and green, shipped in cool conditions to slow them down, and then placed in ripening rooms where a controlled amount of ethylene is added. At home, you can speed up ripening by placing an unripe fruit in a paper bag with a ripe banana, which keeps the gas close to it. Other fruits, such as grapes and strawberries, do not ripen further after picking.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To stop them from producing starch"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To remove ethylene from the fruit"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To slow down ripening during the journey"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To make them sweeter before sale"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Cool shipping slows the fruit down until ethylene is added in ripening rooms.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-08', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Sweeteners found by accident', 'What does the speaker imply about these discoveries?', NULL, 'Several artificial sweeteners were discovered by accident, often because of poor laboratory hygiene. In 1879, the chemist Constantin Fahlberg was working on coal tar compounds at a university in the United States. That evening at dinner, he noticed that his bread tasted unusually sweet. He realised the sweetness came from a chemical on his hands, which he had not washed properly, and traced it back to his laboratory. The substance was saccharin, which is hundreds of times sweeter than sugar. Decades later, a sweetener called cyclamate was found when a student put his cigarette down on a laboratory bench and then noticed a sweet taste. Another sweetener was discovered in the 1970s when a researcher reportedly misheard an instruction to test a compound as an instruction to taste it. Such practices would be considered dangerous in laboratories today.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "They were the result of carefully planned experiments"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They were all made by the same research team"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They produced sweeteners weaker than sugar"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They resulted from practices that would now be considered unsafe"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. The speaker ends by saying such practices would now be considered dangerous.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-09', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The MSG scare', 'What did controlled studies of MSG find?', NULL, 'In 1968, a doctor wrote a letter to a medical journal describing symptoms such as numbness and weakness after eating at Chinese restaurants. He wondered whether monosodium glutamate, or MSG, a flavour enhancer, might be responsible. The idea spread quickly, and a name for the supposed condition entered popular language. Yet when researchers later carried out controlled studies, in which neither participants nor researchers knew who had received MSG, they did not find consistent reactions at the amounts normally found in food. Glutamate is also present naturally in foods such as tomatoes, mushrooms and aged cheese, which many people eat without concern. Some researchers argue that the rapid spread of fears about MSG reflected cultural bias against foreign food as much as scientific evidence. Food safety agencies generally regard MSG as safe, although a small number of people may be sensitive to very large doses.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "No consistent reactions at the amounts normally found in food"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Strong reactions in most people who ate it"}, {"id": "C", "text": "That glutamate does not occur naturally in food"}, {"id": "D", "text": "That the original letter described the wrong symptoms"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Blind studies found no consistent reactions at normal amounts.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-10', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Use-by and best-before dates', 'What is the main difference between the two kinds of date?', NULL, 'Many shoppers throw away food as soon as the date on the packet has passed, but not all dates mean the same thing. In many countries, a use-by date is about safety. It appears on foods that can become dangerous quickly, such as fresh meat and fish, and these foods should not be eaten after that date. A best-before date, by contrast, is about quality. After it, the food may be less crisp or flavourful, but it is usually still safe to eat if it has been stored properly. Surveys suggest that many consumers confuse the two labels, and this confusion contributes to large amounts of household food waste. For this reason, several supermarkets have removed best-before dates from fresh fruit and vegetables altogether, encouraging customers to use their own judgement about whether produce is still good.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "One is for fruit and the other is for meat"}, {"id": "B", "text": "One concerns safety and the other concerns quality"}, {"id": "C", "text": "One is set by shops and the other by governments"}, {"id": "D", "text": "One shows when to buy and the other when to cook"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. Use-by dates are about safety; best-before dates are about quality.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-11', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Eating insects', 'According to the speaker, what is the main barrier to eating insects in many Western countries?', NULL, 'Insects are already a normal part of the diet for an estimated two billion people, particularly in parts of Asia, Africa and Latin America. Crickets, beetle larvae and caterpillars can be rich in protein, healthy fats and minerals. Supporters argue that farming insects could be far more efficient than raising cattle. Insects are cold-blooded, so they do not use energy to keep warm, and they convert feed into body weight much more efficiently. They also need less land and water. In many Western countries, however, the main obstacle is not nutrition or cost but disgust. For this reason, companies selling insect products often grind them into flour and add them to familiar foods, such as pasta, biscuits and protein bars, where the insects cannot be seen. Whether this approach will change eating habits on a large scale remains uncertain.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Their poor nutritional value"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The high cost of farming them"}, {"id": "C", "text": "People''s feelings of disgust"}, {"id": "D", "text": "A shortage of land and water"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. The speaker says the main obstacle is disgust, not nutrition or cost.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-12', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'How soap cleans', 'Why can water alone not remove grease?', NULL, 'Why does soap clean so much better than water alone? Many kinds of dirt, including grease and the oils from our skin, do not mix with water, so rinsing simply slides over them. Soap molecules solve this problem because they have two different ends. One end is attracted to water, while the other avoids water and is attracted to oils and fats. When you wash your hands, the oil-loving ends attach to grease and dirt, and the molecules gather around it in tiny clusters. The water-loving ends face outwards, so the whole cluster can be rinsed away. Soap can also damage certain viruses, which are surrounded by a fatty outer layer that the soap molecules break apart. This is why health authorities recommend washing hands with soap for at least twenty seconds, giving the molecules time to work.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Water damages the skin''s natural oils"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Water is attracted to the oils in dirt"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Grease is protected by a layer of viruses"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Grease does not mix with water"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Grease and oils do not mix with water, so rinsing slides over them.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-13', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Pressure cookers', 'Why does food take longer to cook in boiling water on high mountains?', NULL, 'At sea level, water boils at one hundred degrees Celsius. But the boiling point depends on air pressure, and as you climb higher, the pressure falls. On high mountains, water boils at a noticeably lower temperature, so food cooked in boiling water takes much longer to become tender. Climbers near the summit of Everest would find their water boiling at around seventy degrees, which is not hot enough to cook rice or lentils properly. A pressure cooker solves this problem. Its lid seals tightly, so steam cannot escape, and the pressure inside rises. Under this higher pressure, water can reach well over one hundred degrees before boiling, and food cooks much faster. The principle is old: in the seventeenth century, the French scientist Denis Papin demonstrated a steam digester that could soften even bones. Today pressure cookers are especially popular in mountain regions.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Water boils at a lower temperature there"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Cooking pots lose heat faster in cold air"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The air contains too little oxygen for the fire"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The pressure inside the pot is too high"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Lower air pressure lowers the boiling point, so the water is not as hot.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-14', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Shocks from door handles', 'Why are static shocks more common in winter?', NULL, 'Most people have felt a small electric shock when touching a door handle after walking across a carpet. This is static electricity. When two materials rub together, electrons can move from one to the other, leaving one surface with a negative charge and the other with a positive charge. The charge builds up on your body until you touch a conductor, such as a metal handle, and it suddenly jumps across, producing a tiny spark. Static shocks are much more common in winter. The reason is not the cold itself but the dry air. When the air is humid, a thin layer of moisture on surfaces allows charge to leak away gradually. In dry conditions, charge builds up instead. Static electricity is usually harmless, but it can be dangerous near flammable vapours, which is why fuel stations and factories take care to prevent sparks.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Cold temperatures create more electrons"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The air is drier, so charge cannot leak away"}, {"id": "C", "text": "People touch more metal objects in winter"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Carpets produce more charge when cold"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The speaker says the cause is dry air, not the cold itself.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-15', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Franklin''s rod', 'How does a lightning rod protect a building?', NULL, 'In the 1750s, Benjamin Franklin set out to show that lightning was a form of electricity. In his famous kite experiment, he reportedly flew a kite during a storm and collected electric charge in a special jar, although historians still debate exactly how the experiment was carried out. Whatever the details, Franklin used his ideas to design a practical invention: the lightning rod. A pointed metal rod is fixed to the highest part of a building and connected by a thick wire to the ground. If lightning strikes, the current follows this path into the earth instead of passing through the walls and roof, where it could start a fire. Some people at the time objected, believing that lightning was a divine punishment that humans should not interfere with. Nevertheless, lightning rods spread quickly, and they are still used on tall buildings today.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It stops lightning from forming above the building"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It stores electric charge in a special jar"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It gives the current a safe path to the ground"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It keeps rain away from the roof"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. The current follows the rod and wire into the earth instead of the building.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-16', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The blue LED', 'Why was the blue LED so important?', NULL, 'Light-emitting diodes, or LEDs, have existed since the 1960s, but for decades they were used mainly as small red or green indicator lights. To produce bright white light efficiently, engineers needed a blue LED as well, and this proved extraordinarily difficult. Many large companies gave up. In the late 1980s and early 1990s, three researchers working in Japan, Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano and Shuji Nakamura, finally succeeded, using a material called gallium nitride that others had considered too hard to work with. Their breakthrough made energy-saving white LED lamps possible, as well as the bright screens in modern phones and televisions. LED lamps use a fraction of the electricity of traditional bulbs and last far longer. In 2014, the three scientists were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics, with the committee noting that the invention would benefit the whole world.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It was the first LED ever produced"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It replaced green indicator lights in phones"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It was cheaper to make than red LEDs"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It made efficient white LED lighting possible"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. White LED light needed a blue LED, which the three researchers finally made.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-17', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Why bicycles stay upright', 'What did the 2011 experiment show?', NULL, 'Why does a moving bicycle stay upright, while a stationary one falls over? For many years, the usual explanation was that the spinning wheels act like gyroscopes, resisting any change in their direction. Another popular idea focused on the angle of the front fork. In 2011, however, a team of engineers built an unusual experimental bicycle designed to cancel out both of these effects. Surprisingly, when pushed without a rider, it still balanced itself. The researchers concluded that stability comes from a combination of factors, including how the mass of the bicycle is distributed and how the front wheel is steered. When a bicycle starts to lean, the front wheel tends to turn in the same direction, bringing the wheels back under the frame. A rider does the same thing without thinking, steering slightly into a fall.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "A bicycle can balance itself without the effects once thought essential"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Spinning wheels are the only reason bicycles stay upright"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Riders cannot balance a bicycle without training"}, {"id": "D", "text": "A stationary bicycle is more stable than a moving one"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. The experimental bicycle cancelled the gyroscope and fork effects but still balanced.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-18', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Swerving free kicks', 'What causes a spinning ball to curve?', NULL, 'Footballers who can make the ball swerve in the air are using a principle called the Magnus effect. When a player strikes the ball off-centre, it spins as it travels. The spinning surface drags the surrounding air with it, so air on one side of the ball moves faster than air on the other. This creates a difference in pressure, and the ball is pushed sideways, curving towards the side with lower pressure. One of the most famous examples came in 1997, when the Brazilian defender Roberto Carlos scored a free kick that seemed to be heading well wide before bending sharply into the goal. The same effect explains why tennis players use topspin to make the ball dip, and engineers have even applied it to ships, using tall spinning cylinders on deck to help them move with the wind.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The weight of the ball shifting as it spins"}, {"id": "B", "text": "A difference in air pressure on opposite sides"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Wind blowing across the pitch"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The surface of the ball becoming smoother"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. Faster air on one side creates a pressure difference that pushes the ball sideways.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-19', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Banned swimsuits', 'Why did the governing body change the rules in 2010?', NULL, 'At the Beijing Olympics in 2008 and the world championships a year later, swimmers broke world records at an astonishing rate. Much of the credit went to a new generation of full-body swimsuits. Some were made partly or entirely from polyurethane, a material that trapped air, increased buoyancy and squeezed the body into a more streamlined shape, reducing drag in the water. Critics argued that races were becoming contests between suit manufacturers rather than athletes, and that swimmers without the latest suits were at a disadvantage. In response, the sport''s governing body introduced new rules from 2010, requiring suits to be made from textile materials and limiting how much of the body they could cover. Many records set in that period stood for years afterwards, and some commentators still describe them as difficult to compare with those set before or since.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Because the suits caused injuries to swimmers"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Because the suits were too expensive for most teams"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To stop swimsuit technology from deciding results"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To encourage more swimmers to break records"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Critics said races were becoming contests between suit makers.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-20', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Super shoes', 'How did the governing body respond to the new shoes?', NULL, 'In recent years, running shoes with a stiff carbon-fibre plate inside thick, springy foam have transformed long-distance running. Laboratory studies found that some of these shoes reduced the energy runners needed at a given pace by around four percent on average, a very large improvement at elite level. Marathon times fell sharply, and athletes wearing them set a series of records. The shoes raised a difficult question: at what point does equipment give an unfair advantage? In 2020, the sport''s governing body responded not by banning the technology but by setting limits. Shoes used in road races must now have soles no thicker than forty millimetres, and they must be available for the public to buy, so that athletes cannot use secret prototypes. Some experts believe the benefit varies considerably from one runner to another.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "By banning carbon plates from all races"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By removing records set in the shoes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By giving identical shoes to every athlete"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By limiting sole thickness and requiring the shoes to be on sale"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. The body set limits rather than banning the technology.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-21', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Training in thin air', 'What is the purpose of the live high, train low approach?', NULL, 'Many endurance athletes spend weeks training at high altitude before important competitions. At altitude, each breath contains less oxygen, and over several weeks the body responds by producing more red blood cells, which carry oxygen to the muscles. When the athletes return to lower ground, they may benefit from this extra capacity for a limited time. However, training hard at altitude is difficult, because athletes cannot run as fast in the thin air. This led to an approach known as live high, train low: athletes sleep and rest at altitude but travel down to lower areas for their most intense sessions. The success of runners who grew up in the highlands of Kenya and Ethiopia has also drawn attention to altitude, though researchers stress that many other factors, including culture and opportunity, contribute to their achievements.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To gain the benefits of altitude while still training at high intensity"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To avoid producing extra red blood cells"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To reduce the time athletes spend travelling"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To copy the diet of runners from Kenya"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Athletes live at altitude but go lower for their hardest sessions.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-22', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Goalkeepers and penalties', 'According to the researchers, why do goalkeepers usually dive?', NULL, 'A study of penalty kicks in top football leagues found something surprising about goalkeepers. When a penalty is taken, goalkeepers dive to the left or right almost every time, rarely staying in the centre of the goal. Yet the data suggested that staying in the centre gave them a chance of stopping the ball that was at least as good as diving. Why, then, do they dive? The researchers proposed an explanation called action bias. If a goal is conceded while the goalkeeper stays still, it looks as though he did nothing, and he is likely to feel worse and receive more criticism. If he dives and the ball goes the other way, he at least appears to have tried. In other words, the choice may be shaped less by the best chance of saving the ball than by how the outcome will look to others.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Most penalties are kicked towards the corners"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Doing nothing looks worse if a goal is conceded"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Staying in the centre gives the lowest chance of a save"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Coaches punish goalkeepers who do not move"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The action bias explanation: standing still looks like doing nothing.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-23', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Riding in the peloton', 'Why are breakaway groups often caught before the finish?', NULL, 'In professional cycling races, riders travel in a large, tightly packed group called the peloton. The main reason is air resistance, which accounts for most of the effort a cyclist makes at racing speeds. A rider directly behind another experiences much less drag, and computer simulations have suggested that riders in the middle of a large peloton may face only a small fraction of the air resistance of a cyclist riding alone. This has a strong influence on tactics. Teams place riders around their leader to shelter him from the wind and save his energy for the decisive moments of the race. It also explains why small groups that break away from the peloton are so often caught before the finish: a large group sharing the work at the front can travel faster for longer than a few riders on their own.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Breakaway riders are usually less experienced"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Race rules require riders to stay together"}, {"id": "C", "text": "A large group can share the work of facing the wind"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Small groups face less air resistance"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. The larger group shares the effort at the front and goes faster for longer.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-24', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The smell of swimming pools', 'What does a strong chlorine smell usually indicate?', NULL, 'Many people assume that a strong smell of chlorine at a swimming pool means the water is especially clean. In fact, the opposite is often true. Chlorine is added to pools to kill harmful microorganisms, but when it reacts with substances brought in by swimmers, such as sweat, skin oils and urine, it forms compounds called chloramines. It is mainly these chloramines that produce the sharp smell and can irritate the eyes and lungs. A well-managed pool, with good ventilation and the correct balance of chemicals, should have only a faint smell. Pool operators therefore encourage swimmers to shower before entering the water, which reduces the amount of material that reacts with chlorine. So the next time your eyes sting after a swim, it may be a sign that the pool needs more attention, not less.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The water contains more chlorine than necessary"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The pool has just been cleaned"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The ventilation is working well"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Chlorine has reacted with substances from swimmers"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. The smell comes mainly from chloramines formed with sweat, oils and urine.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-25', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Head injuries in sport', 'Why have some football competitions introduced extra substitutions for concussion?', NULL, 'For many years, players who received a blow to the head were often encouraged to carry on, and being knocked out briefly was sometimes treated as part of the game. Attitudes have changed as evidence has grown that repeated head injuries can have serious long-term effects on the brain. Many sports now have formal concussion procedures. In rugby, a player with a suspected head injury may be removed for an assessment by a doctor, and a replacement can come on while this takes place. Several football competitions now allow extra substitutions specifically for concussion, so that teams are not tempted to keep an injured player on the field to avoid being a player short. At amateur level, where doctors are rarely present, the advice is simpler: if in doubt, sit them out.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "So teams are not tempted to keep injured players on the field"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To give more young players a chance to play"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Because a doctor must attend every match"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To make matches last longer"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Extra substitutions remove the pressure to avoid being a player short.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-26', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'reCAPTCHA', 'What was the main idea behind reCAPTCHA?', NULL, 'You have probably been asked to type distorted letters or choose pictures of traffic lights before logging in to a website. These tests, known as CAPTCHAs, are designed to tell humans apart from automated programs. The computer scientist Luis von Ahn, one of their inventors, realised that people around the world were collectively spending an enormous amount of time solving them, and he wondered whether this effort could be put to use. His answer was reCAPTCHA. Instead of random letters, users were shown words from old books and newspapers that computers had failed to read when the pages were scanned. Each time someone typed the word correctly, they were helping to digitise printed texts. Later versions, showing photographs of street scenes, have been used to help train computer systems to recognise objects.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To make logging in faster for users"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To use people''s effort to help digitise printed texts"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To stop people reading old newspapers online"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To measure how quickly people can type"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. Users typed words from scanned books that computers could not read.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-27', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Why spam pays', 'According to the study, why does spam remain profitable?', NULL, 'Why do spammers keep sending millions of unwanted emails when almost nobody responds? In 2008, a group of computer scientists studied this question by quietly taking control of part of a network used by spammers. They tracked a campaign that sent around three hundred and fifty million emails advertising medicines. Only twenty-eight people went on to make a purchase, a response rate of well under one in ten million. At first glance, this seems like a failure. But sending an email costs the spammer almost nothing, especially when the messages are sent from thousands of infected computers belonging to other people. Even a tiny number of sales can therefore produce a profit. The researchers concluded that spam is sustained by extremely low costs rather than by success in persuading people, and later research suggested targeting the banks that spammers depend on.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Most people who receive it make a purchase"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Spammers are paid by advertising companies"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Sending messages costs almost nothing"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The emails are sent only to wealthy customers"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Very low costs mean even twenty-eight sales can make a profit.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-28', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'New password advice', 'What did the revised guidance emphasise?', NULL, 'For years, organisations told users to create passwords with a mixture of capital letters, numbers and symbols, and to change them every few months. In 2017, the United States National Institute of Standards and Technology revised its widely followed guidance. It concluded that these rules often made security worse. Complicated requirements led people to choose predictable patterns, such as replacing a letter with a number, and frequent changes encouraged them to make only tiny adjustments or to write passwords down. The new advice emphasised length instead. A long passphrase made of several unrelated words can be both easier to remember and harder to guess. The guidance also recommended checking new passwords against lists of passwords already exposed in data breaches, and forcing a change only if there is evidence that a password has been compromised.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Changing passwords every few months"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Replacing letters with numbers"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Writing passwords down in a safe place"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Making passwords longer rather than more complicated"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. The new advice emphasised length, such as long passphrases.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-29', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Website cookies', 'Why were cookies first introduced?', NULL, 'A cookie is a small text file that a website stores on your device. Cookies were introduced in 1994 by a programmer working on an early web browser, who needed a way for an online shop to remember what a customer had put in their basket from one page to the next. Many cookies are still used for such practical purposes, keeping you logged in or remembering your language settings. However, so-called third-party cookies, placed by companies other than the website you are visiting, allow advertisers to follow your activity across many different sites and build a detailed profile of your interests. Concerns about privacy led the European Union to require websites to ask for consent before storing most non-essential cookies, which is why so many sites now show pop-up banners. Several browsers also block third-party cookies by default.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To let an online shop remember a customer''s basket"}, {"id": "B", "text": "To let advertisers track users across websites"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To meet European privacy laws"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To block unwanted pop-up banners"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. The first cookies helped a shop remember the basket between pages.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-30', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Predictive text', 'How do modern predictive keyboards decide which word to suggest?', NULL, 'When you type a message on a phone, the keyboard often suggests the next word before you have started it. Early mobile phones used a system called T9, which guessed which word you meant from a sequence of number key presses. Modern predictive keyboards work by calculating probabilities. They are built using enormous collections of text, from which they learn which words are most likely to follow others, and they also adapt to the words you personally use most often. This makes typing faster, but it has side effects. Autocorrect sometimes replaces an unusual but correct word, such as a name, with a more common one, occasionally with embarrassing results. Some linguists also wonder whether constant suggestions might gradually encourage people to use more predictable language, although the long-term effects are not yet clear.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "By counting number key presses"}, {"id": "B", "text": "By calculating which words are most likely to follow others"}, {"id": "C", "text": "By checking a dictionary of correct spellings"}, {"id": "D", "text": "By asking the user to choose a language"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. They learn word probabilities from large collections of text.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-31', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Machine translation', 'According to the speaker, why do some languages remain difficult for machine translation?', NULL, 'In 1954, researchers in the United States demonstrated a computer that translated about sixty Russian sentences into English, and some predicted that machine translation would be solved within a few years. It took far longer. Early systems relied on rules written by linguists, but human language is full of ambiguity, and rules could not cover every case. A major change came when researchers began using statistics, training systems on huge collections of documents that had already been translated by humans, such as the records of international organisations. Since the mid-2010s, systems based on neural networks have produced far more fluent results. Yet quality still varies enormously between languages. Translation between widely used languages, for which vast amounts of text exist, is often very good, while languages with little available written material remain much harder for machines.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Their grammar has too many rules"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Linguists have not written rules for them"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Little written material exists for training systems"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They were left out of the 1954 demonstration"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Systems learn from existing text, so languages with little text are harder.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-32', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Drones carrying blood', 'How are the packages delivered to hospitals?', NULL, 'In 2016, Rwanda became the first country to use drones for regular deliveries of blood to hospitals. The service was run by a company called Zipline, working with the national health system. Blood is difficult to manage in rural areas. It must be stored carefully, some types expire quickly, and patients who need it after childbirth or accidents often cannot wait. Previously, a clinic might have waited hours for a delivery by road. Using small fixed-wing drones, the company could deliver within about half an hour. The drones do not land. Instead, they fly low over the hospital and drop the package, which floats down on a small paper parachute, before returning to base. The model has since been extended to vaccines and other medical supplies, and to other countries.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The drones land on hospital roofs"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They are collected by motorcycle from a base"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They are carried the last part of the way by road"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They are dropped by parachute from the drone"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. The drones fly low and drop packages on paper parachutes.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-33', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Metals in e-waste', 'What example of urban mining does the speaker give?', NULL, 'Old mobile phones, laptops and other electronic devices contain small amounts of valuable metals, including gold, silver and copper. In fact, a tonne of discarded phones can contain far more gold than a tonne of ore from a typical gold mine. Recovering these metals from waste is sometimes called urban mining. A striking example came from Japan, which made all the medals for the Tokyo Olympic Games from metals extracted from electronic devices donated by the public over two years. Despite such projects, only a minority of the world''s electronic waste is formally collected and recycled. Much of the rest ends up in landfill or is processed informally, sometimes by burning cables and circuit boards in the open air, which exposes workers to toxic fumes. Better design, making devices easier to take apart, could make recycling safer and more profitable.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Olympic medals made from recycled electronics"}, {"id": "B", "text": "A new gold mine opened in Japan"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Phones redesigned to contain no metal"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Landfill sites turned into mines"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Japan made the Tokyo medals from metals in donated devices.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-34', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Server farms', 'What happened in the underwater experiment?', NULL, 'The data centres that store our emails, photographs and videos contain thousands of computer servers running day and night. These machines produce a great deal of heat, and keeping them cool can use a large share of a data centre''s total energy. Companies have tried several solutions. Some have built centres in cold countries, such as Finland and Sweden, where outside air can be used for cooling for much of the year. Others use nearby seawater or lakes. In one experiment, a technology company sealed servers inside a container and placed it on the seabed off Scotland for two years; the servers failed less often than similar ones on land, perhaps because of the stable temperature and the absence of people. Increasingly, data centres are also connected to local heating networks, so that their waste heat can warm homes.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The servers overheated in the seawater"}, {"id": "B", "text": "The servers failed less often than similar ones on land"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The container was later moved to Finland"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The servers heated homes on the coast"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The sealed servers on the seabed failed less often.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-35', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Wrist heart-rate sensors', 'Why do some athletes still use chest straps?', NULL, 'Many smartwatches and fitness bands can measure your heart rate without any needles or wires. On the underside of the device, small lights, usually green, shine into the skin of your wrist. Blood absorbs green light, so the amount of light reflected back changes slightly as blood pulses through the vessels with each heartbeat. A sensor detects these changes, and software converts them into a heart rate. The method works well when the wearer is resting, but it can become less reliable during vigorous exercise, when arm movements disturb the signal, or if the watch is worn loosely. Some studies have also found that accuracy can vary with skin tone and tattoos. For this reason, many athletes who need precise measurements still use a strap worn around the chest, which detects the heart''s electrical activity directly.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Smartwatches cannot measure heart rate at rest"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Chest straps use green light instead of electricity"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They measure the heart''s electrical activity more precisely"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Wrist devices damage the skin during exercise"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Chest straps detect the heart''s electrical signals directly.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-36', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'How Wikipedia works', 'What advice do teachers often give about Wikipedia?', NULL, 'When Wikipedia was launched in 2001, many people doubted that an encyclopedia anyone could edit would ever be reliable. Yet it grew rapidly into one of the most visited websites in the world. Its model depends on large numbers of volunteers who write articles, check sources and correct errors. Key rules require information to be supported by published sources and articles to be written from a neutral point of view. Deliberate damage, known as vandalism, is common, but it is often removed within minutes, partly by automated programs. In 2005, the journal Nature compared a selection of science articles with those in another well-known encyclopedia and reported a broadly similar number of serious errors, though the other publisher strongly disputed the study. Teachers often advise students to use Wikipedia as a starting point and to follow its references, rather than citing it directly.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Avoid it completely because of vandalism"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Cite it directly in academic essays"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Use it only for science topics"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Use it as a starting point and follow its references"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Teachers suggest starting there and following the references.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-37', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The War of the Worlds broadcast', 'According to later researchers, why might newspapers have exaggerated the panic?', NULL, 'On the evening of 30 October 1938, an American radio station broadcast a drama based on H. G. Wells''s novel The War of the Worlds, presented as a series of realistic news reports about a Martian invasion. The next day, newspapers reported that the programme had caused mass panic, with frightened listeners fleeing their homes. The story became famous as an example of the power of the media. However, later researchers have argued that the panic was greatly exaggerated. Relatively few people were listening to the programme, and there is little evidence of widespread flight. Some historians suggest that newspapers had a reason to exaggerate: radio was a new and growing competitor for advertising, and reports of irresponsible broadcasting helped to portray it as dangerous. Ironically, the myth of the panic may itself show how easily media stories spread.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Radio was competing with them for advertising"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Most journalists had listened to the programme"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The radio station paid them for publicity"}, {"id": "D", "text": "The government wanted to ban radio dramas"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Radio was a new rival for advertising, so newspapers had a motive.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-38', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The rise of podcasts', 'What does the speaker identify as the main challenge for podcast producers now?', NULL, 'The word podcast was coined in 2004 by a journalist who combined the name of a popular music player with the word broadcast. At first, podcasts were a niche interest, downloaded by relatively small numbers of enthusiasts. Growth accelerated around 2014, when a true-crime series called Serial attracted millions of listeners and showed that audio storytelling could become a mass phenomenon. Since then, the number of podcasts has grown enormously. Part of their appeal is convenience: listeners can choose exactly what they want and listen while commuting, exercising or doing housework. For producers, costs are low compared with radio or television, so it is possible to serve small, specialised audiences, from fans of medieval history to people learning a particular language. The challenge now is not making a podcast but getting anyone to notice it among so many.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The high cost of recording equipment"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Attracting attention among so many podcasts"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Finding listeners who commute"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Getting permission to use the name podcast"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The speaker says the challenge is getting noticed among so many.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-39', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'False stories on Twitter', 'What did the study find about bots?', NULL, 'In 2018, researchers published a large study of how true and false news stories spread on Twitter between 2006 and 2017. They examined around one hundred and twenty-six thousand stories that had been checked by independent fact-checking organisations. False stories spread further, faster and more widely than true ones, and this was especially the case for false political news. Many people assume automated accounts, or bots, are mainly responsible, but the researchers found that bots spread true and false news at similar rates. The difference was driven by human users. One possible explanation is novelty: false stories were more often surprising, and people like to share information that seems new. The false stories also tended to provoke stronger emotional reactions, such as surprise and disgust. The findings suggest that fighting misinformation requires changing human behaviour, not just removing automated accounts.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "They were responsible for most false news"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They mainly shared political stories"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They spread true and false stories at similar rates"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They were removed by fact-checking organisations"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. The difference in spread came from human users, not bots.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-40', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Altered photographs', 'What does the speaker say about news agencies today?', NULL, 'Many people think that faked photographs are a product of the digital age, but images have been altered almost since photography began. One of the best-known examples comes from the Soviet Union in the 1930s. When officials fell out of favour with Joseph Stalin, they were sometimes removed from official photographs, so that it appeared they had never been present. In one famous case, a senior police official who had been photographed walking beside Stalin simply vanished from later versions of the picture. These changes were made by hand in darkrooms, using techniques such as painting over prints and cutting negatives. Digital software has made alteration much easier, which is why many news agencies now have strict rules forbidding any change to the content of a news photograph beyond basic adjustments such as brightness.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "They use darkroom techniques to check images"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They allow officials to be removed from pictures"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They no longer publish photographs of politicians"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Many forbid changing the content of news photographs"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Agencies allow only basic adjustments such as brightness.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-41', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Recorded laughter on television', 'Why did laugh tracks remain in use for so long?', NULL, 'For decades, television comedies were often accompanied by recorded laughter, known as a laugh track. In the 1950s, a sound engineer named Charles Douglass built a machine containing recordings of different kinds of laughter, from gentle chuckles to loud roars, which he could play at chosen moments. Producers used it to add laughter to shows filmed without an audience, or to boost reactions that seemed too quiet. Many viewers found laugh tracks irritating, yet there was a reason they persisted. Research suggests that laughter is contagious: people tend to laugh more, and rate jokes as funnier, when they hear others laughing. Television producers were, in effect, providing a virtual audience for people watching alone at home. In recent years, many popular comedies have abandoned laugh tracks, trusting viewers to decide for themselves what is funny.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Hearing laughter makes people find jokes funnier"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Viewers asked producers to include them"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Live audiences were not allowed in studios"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They were cheaper than writing new jokes"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. Laughter is contagious, so the track made shows seem funnier.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-42', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Trailers', 'Why were trailers moved to the beginning of the programme?', NULL, 'The short advertisements for upcoming films are called trailers, which seems strange, because today they are shown before the main feature. The name comes from the early days of cinema, when these advertisements were shown after the film had finished, trailing behind it. Cinema owners soon noticed that many people left as soon as the main film ended, so the trailers were moved to the beginning of the programme, where the audience was certain to see them. The name, however, remained. Trailers have since become an art form of their own. Editors must attract attention in two or three minutes without revealing too much of the story, and studios often test several versions with audiences before choosing one. With the rise of online video, the release of a trailer for a major film has become a news event in itself.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Audiences complained that they revealed the ending"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Many viewers left as soon as the film ended"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Films became too long to include them afterwards"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Online video made them more popular"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. Audiences left when the main film finished, so trailers were moved.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-43', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Competitive gaming', 'What does the speaker say about esports careers?', NULL, 'Competitive video gaming, known as esports, has grown from small gatherings of enthusiasts into a global industry. Major tournaments now fill large arenas, and millions more watch online. Professional players often train for many hours a day, practising strategies with their teams and studying opponents, much like athletes in traditional sports. Whether esports should count as sport remains a subject of debate. Critics argue that they lack the physical effort that defines sport, while supporters point to the fast reactions, precise hand movements and intense concentration they demand. The question gained new attention when esports were included as official medal events at the Asian Games held in China in 2023. Despite the attention, careers can be very short. Many professional players retire in their mid-twenties, partly because of the pressure of constant competition and the physical strain of long hours of practice.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "They usually last longer than careers in traditional sports"}, {"id": "B", "text": "They depend mainly on physical strength"}, {"id": "C", "text": "They are often very short"}, {"id": "D", "text": "They usually begin after university"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Many players retire in their mid-twenties.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-44', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The Jazz Singer', 'According to the speaker, which group lost their jobs because of sound films?', NULL, 'In 1927, the film The Jazz Singer included sequences of synchronised speech and song, and audiences were amazed to hear an actor speak on screen. Within a few years, silent films had almost disappeared. The change transformed the industry in ways that went beyond sound itself. Some famous silent stars found their careers in difficulty, because their voices or accents did not suit the roles they were known for, or because they struggled with dialogue. Early sound equipment also created technical problems: noisy cameras had to be placed inside soundproof booths, which made filming less flexible for a time. Another group lost out as well. Many cinemas had employed musicians to play live accompaniment for silent films, and thousands of these jobs vanished as recorded soundtracks took over.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Camera operators in film studios"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Writers of silent film scripts"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Engineers who built sound equipment"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Musicians who played in cinemas"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Live accompaniment was replaced by recorded soundtracks.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-45', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Inventing the piano', 'What was the key advantage of Cristofori''s instrument?', NULL, 'Before the piano, the most common keyboard instrument in European homes and courts was the harpsichord. When a harpsichord key is pressed, a small mechanism plucks the string, and the volume is much the same however hard the key is struck. Around the year 1700, Bartolomeo Cristofori, an instrument maker in Florence, developed a new mechanism in which small hammers struck the strings and then immediately fell back, allowing them to vibrate. Crucially, the harder the player pressed the key, the louder the sound. His instrument was described as a harpsichord with soft and loud, which in Italian included the words piano and forte. The name was eventually shortened to piano. The new instrument took decades to become popular, but composers such as Mozart and Beethoven later wrote music that depended on its range of expression.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Players could control how loud each note was"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It was much cheaper than a harpsichord"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Its strings were plucked more gently"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It could easily be carried between rooms"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. The harder the key was pressed, the louder the sound.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-46', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Tuning an orchestra', 'Why do orchestras tune to the oboe?', NULL, 'Before a concert, an orchestra tunes to a single note played by the oboe. There are practical reasons for this. The oboe has a clear, penetrating sound that can be heard across the whole orchestra, and its pitch is relatively stable. The note chosen is usually an A. For much of history, the exact pitch of this A varied considerably from place to place, so an instrument tuned in one city might sound out of tune in another. In the twentieth century, a standard was widely adopted in which the A vibrates four hundred and forty times per second. Even so, the standard is not followed everywhere. Many orchestras tune slightly higher, believing it gives a brighter sound, while groups performing music from earlier centuries often tune lower to recreate the pitch the composers would have known.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It is the loudest instrument in the orchestra"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Its sound is clear and its pitch is stable"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It can easily change pitch to match others"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It plays the lowest note in the orchestra"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. The oboe''s clear sound carries and its pitch is stable.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-47', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Ultramarine blue', 'Why did contracts sometimes specify how much ultramarine an artist should use?', NULL, 'In Renaissance Europe, the most precious blue pigment was ultramarine. Its name means beyond the sea, because the stone it was made from, lapis lazuli, came from mines in what is now Afghanistan, thousands of kilometres away. Turning the stone into a pure, intense blue was a slow and difficult process: it had to be ground and repeatedly washed to separate the blue particles from impurities. The finished pigment could be more expensive than gold, and contracts for paintings sometimes specified exactly how much ultramarine an artist was to use. Painters often reserved it for the most important figures, such as the robes of the Virgin Mary. In the 1820s, a French society offered a prize for a synthetic version, and a manufacturing method was soon found. Artificial ultramarine was far cheaper, and the colour became available to everyone.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It faded quickly if too much was used"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It was reserved by law for religious paintings"}, {"id": "C", "text": "The pigment was extremely expensive"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Artists preferred the cheaper synthetic blue"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct. Ultramarine could cost more than gold.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-48', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'The Vermeer forger', 'Why did Van Meegeren admit to forgery?', NULL, 'After the Second World War, Dutch authorities discovered that a painting believed to be by Johannes Vermeer had been sold to the senior Nazi Hermann Göring. They traced the sale to a painter named Han van Meegeren and arrested him for selling a national treasure to the enemy, a serious crime. Van Meegeren''s defence was astonishing. He claimed that the painting was not a Vermeer at all, but a forgery he had painted himself. To prove it, he painted another work in the style of Vermeer in front of witnesses. He was cleared of the more serious charge but convicted of forgery. Experts were embarrassed, because several of his paintings had been accepted as genuine and displayed in major museums. His case is often cited as a warning that experts can be misled when a discovery confirms what they hope to find.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "To earn money from museums"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Because experts had already exposed him"}, {"id": "C", "text": "To protect Vermeer''s reputation"}, {"id": "D", "text": "To avoid a more serious charge"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct. Admitting forgery cleared him of selling a national treasure to the enemy.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-49', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Cinderella around the world', 'What does the speaker say about the glass slipper?', NULL, 'The story of Cinderella is often associated with a single famous version, but folklorists have recorded hundreds of related tales from around the world. One of the oldest written versions comes from China, in a collection from the ninth century, in which a young woman named Ye Xian is helped by a magical fish and loses a golden shoe. An even earlier story, recorded by a Greek writer, tells of a woman whose sandal is carried off by an eagle and dropped in front of a king. Many details that people now consider essential, such as the fairy godmother, the pumpkin and the glass slipper, come from a French version published in 1697. By comparing such variations, folklorists try to understand how stories travel between cultures and which elements survive as they are retold.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "It comes from a French version published in 1697"}, {"id": "B", "text": "It appears in the oldest Chinese version"}, {"id": "C", "text": "It was carried off by an eagle"}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is found in every version of the story"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct. The slipper, godmother and pumpkin come from the 1697 French version.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-mcsa-50', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Shakespeare''s vocabulary', 'What have digital collections of early books revealed?', NULL, 'William Shakespeare is often credited with inventing a remarkable number of English words and expressions. Dictionaries have long listed his plays as the earliest known source for many terms, and phrases such as wild-goose chase are first recorded in his work. However, recent research using large digital collections of early printed books has complicated this picture. Scholars searching these collections have found that many words once credited to Shakespeare were in fact used by other writers before him. Because his works were studied so carefully, they were simply the first place dictionary makers looked. This does not mean his influence was small. He clearly created some words and gave new life to many others, but it suggests that the language of his time was more inventive than a focus on one writer might suggest.', 5, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Shakespeare invented more words than previously thought"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Many words credited to Shakespeare were used earlier by others"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Dictionary makers ignored Shakespeare''s plays"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Shakespeare never used the phrase wild-goose chase"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct. Searches found earlier uses of many supposed Shakespeare coinages.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'MCQ Single Answer']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Unfinished tasks', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In the 1920s, the psychologist Bluma Zeigarnik noticed that waiters could remember complicated orders until the bill was paid, and then quickly forgot them. Her experiments suggested that people remember interrupted tasks better than completed ones. In other words, an unfinished task tends to stay [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "on our minds"}, {"id": "B", "text": "easy to ignore"}, {"id": "C", "text": "in the past"}, {"id": "D", "text": "forgotten quickly"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "on our minds". Interrupted tasks are remembered better, so they stay on our minds.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Liking the familiar', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Studies by the psychologist Robert Zajonc found that people rated unfamiliar shapes, words and faces more positively after seeing them several times, even when they could not remember seeing them before. This is known as the mere exposure effect: simple familiarity tends to increase our [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "fear of something"}, {"id": "B", "text": "liking for something"}, {"id": "C", "text": "boredom with it"}, {"id": "D", "text": "memory of the source"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "liking for something". Repeated exposure made people rate things more positively.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Missing a swap', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In a well-known experiment, a researcher asked a stranger for directions. While they talked, two people carrying a door walked between them, and the researcher was replaced by a different person. About half of the strangers did not notice the switch. The study shows that we often fail to detect large changes when our attention is [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "free to wander"}, {"id": "B", "text": "completely undivided"}, {"id": "C", "text": "focused elsewhere"}, {"id": "D", "text": "on the person''s face"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "focused elsewhere". People missed the change because their attention was on the directions.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-04', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The cost of interruptions', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Research on office workers has found that after being interrupted, people can take a considerable time to return fully to what they were doing. Constant notifications from email and messaging apps therefore cost more than the few seconds needed to read them. For this reason, some experts recommend checking messages only at set times of day, so that people can spend longer periods [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "answering every message"}, {"id": "B", "text": "sitting in meetings"}, {"id": "C", "text": "reading notifications"}, {"id": "D", "text": "working without interruption"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "working without interruption". Checking messages at set times protects long periods of uninterrupted work.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-05', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Halo judgements', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'The halo effect occurs when one positive quality, such as physical attractiveness, influences our judgement of a person''s other qualities. In studies, people rated as attractive were also assumed to be more intelligent and more honest, even though there was no evidence for this. Our first impression can therefore spread to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "unrelated characteristics"}, {"id": "B", "text": "no other judgement"}, {"id": "C", "text": "only their appearance"}, {"id": "D", "text": "people we have never met"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "unrelated characteristics". One positive quality colours judgements of other, unrelated qualities.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-06', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Crossing buttons', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In many cities, some of the buttons that pedestrians press at crossings do not actually change the timing of the lights, especially at busy times when signals are controlled by a central computer. Even so, the buttons are often left in place. Pressing one gives people a sense of control, and researchers suggest this may make them more willing to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "cross against the red light"}, {"id": "B", "text": "wait for the green signal"}, {"id": "C", "text": "complain to the council"}, {"id": "D", "text": "walk a different way home"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "wait for the green signal". A sense of control makes waiting easier, not crossing early.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-07', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Pulling on a rope', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In the nineteenth century, the engineer Max Ringelmann found that when people pulled on a rope in a group, each person pulled less hard than when pulling alone. Later researchers called this social loafing. It is reduced when each person''s contribution can be measured separately, because people work harder when their efforts are [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "shared equally"}, {"id": "B", "text": "hidden from others"}, {"id": "C", "text": "individually visible"}, {"id": "D", "text": "not recorded"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "individually visible". Loafing falls when each person''s effort can be measured, so visible effort is the key.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-08', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'An embarrassing T-shirt', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In one study, students were asked to wear an embarrassing T-shirt into a room full of classmates. They guessed that about half of the people present would notice it, but in reality only around a quarter did. Psychologists call this the spotlight effect: we tend to overestimate how much other people [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "care about fashion"}, {"id": "B", "text": "remember their own clothes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "trust our judgement"}, {"id": "D", "text": "notice about us"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "notice about us". Students expected far more people to notice them than actually did.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-09', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Survival or mortality', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Doctors and patients can make different choices depending on how information is presented. In one classic study, more people chose a surgical treatment when told it had a ninety percent survival rate than when told it had a ten percent mortality rate. The facts were identical; only the [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "wording had changed"}, {"id": "B", "text": "risks had increased"}, {"id": "C", "text": "patients were different"}, {"id": "D", "text": "treatment was cheaper"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "wording had changed". Both descriptions give the same facts in different words.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-10', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Knowing it all along', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'After an event has happened, people often feel that they knew the outcome all along. Election results, sporting upsets and financial crises all seem obvious once we know how they turned out. This tendency, called hindsight bias, can make us overconfident, because it makes the past seem much more [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "confusing than the present"}, {"id": "B", "text": "predictable than it really was"}, {"id": "C", "text": "distant than it is"}, {"id": "D", "text": "exciting than it was"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "predictable than it really was". Hindsight makes outcomes feel obvious, that is, more predictable.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-11', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Workers under observation', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In the 1920s and 1930s, researchers at the Hawthorne factory near Chicago studied how changes in lighting affected workers'' productivity. Output seemed to rise whatever the researchers changed. One popular interpretation is that the workers performed better simply because they knew they were being [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "paid more"}, {"id": "B", "text": "replaced"}, {"id": "C", "text": "observed"}, {"id": "D", "text": "ignored"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "observed". Output rose whatever changed, which was put down to the attention of being studied.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-12', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Naming the ink colour', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In the Stroop test, people are shown colour words, such as red, printed in a different ink, such as blue, and asked to name the ink colour. Most people are noticeably slower when the word and the colour do not match. This happens because, for fluent readers, reading a word is so automatic that it is difficult to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "pronounce"}, {"id": "B", "text": "see"}, {"id": "C", "text": "remember"}, {"id": "D", "text": "ignore"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "ignore". Automatic reading interferes with naming the colour, so the word is hard to ignore.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-13', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Grouping information', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Most people can hold only a small number of separate items in short-term memory at once. However, we can remember far more if we group information into meaningful units. For example, a long telephone number is easier to recall when it is broken into three shorter parts. Psychologists call this process [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "chunking"}, {"id": "B", "text": "forgetting"}, {"id": "C", "text": "rehearsing silently"}, {"id": "D", "text": "multitasking"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "chunking". Grouping items into units is called chunking.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-14', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Sunk costs', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Imagine you have paid for a concert ticket, but on the night you feel ill and would rather stay at home. Many people go anyway, because they do not want to waste the money. Economists point out that the money is already spent whatever you decide, so a rational choice should depend only on [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "how much the ticket cost"}, {"id": "B", "text": "future costs and benefits"}, {"id": "C", "text": "what your friends think"}, {"id": "D", "text": "the price of the next concert"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "future costs and benefits". Money already spent cannot be recovered, so only what lies ahead should count.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-15', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Twenty-six blacks in a row', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'At a casino in Monte Carlo in 1913, the ball in a roulette game landed on black twenty-six times in a row. Gamblers lost huge sums betting on red, believing it was due. But a roulette wheel has no memory; each spin is independent, so the chance of red on the next spin was [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "much higher than usual"}, {"id": "B", "text": "almost zero"}, {"id": "C", "text": "exactly the same as before"}, {"id": "D", "text": "impossible to calculate"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "exactly the same as before". Independent spins mean the odds never change.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-16', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Reusing towels', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Hotels often ask guests to reuse their towels to protect the environment. In one experiment, a card saying that most guests who had stayed in the same room reused their towels was more effective than a card focused only on the environment. People are strongly influenced by what [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "the hotel manager says"}, {"id": "B", "text": "the law requires"}, {"id": "C", "text": "costs the least"}, {"id": "D", "text": "others like them do"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "others like them do". The message about other guests worked best.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-17', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Goldfish and attention', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'You may have heard that human attention spans are now shorter than those of goldfish, at around eight seconds. The claim spread widely through news articles, but researchers have been unable to find the original study, and experts say attention depends heavily on the task. The famous comparison is therefore [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "not supported by evidence"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a proven scientific fact"}, {"id": "C", "text": "true only for children"}, {"id": "D", "text": "based on goldfish research"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "not supported by evidence". No original study can be found, so the claim lacks evidence.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-18', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'More users, more usefulness', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Some products become more valuable as more people use them. A telephone is useless if nobody else has one, but extremely useful when everyone does. The same is true of messaging apps and online marketplaces. Economists call these network effects, and they help explain why a few large platforms often [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "fail within a year"}, {"id": "B", "text": "dominate their markets"}, {"id": "C", "text": "charge nothing forever"}, {"id": "D", "text": "avoid new customers"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "dominate their markets". The more users a platform has, the stronger it becomes, so large platforms dominate.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-19', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Economies of scale', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'A factory that makes a million cars can usually produce each one more cheaply than a factory that makes a thousand. Fixed costs, such as machinery and research, are spread over many more units, and large firms can negotiate lower prices from suppliers. These advantages are known as economies of [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "speed"}, {"id": "B", "text": "design"}, {"id": "C", "text": "scale"}, {"id": "D", "text": "choice"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "scale". Cost advantages from size are economies of scale.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-20', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The cost of a choice', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'When you spend an evening studying, the cost is not only the price of your books. It is also the value of the best alternative you gave up, such as working a paid shift or spending time with friends. Economists call this the opportunity cost, and it reminds us that every choice involves [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "spending money"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a written contract"}, {"id": "C", "text": "a guaranteed reward"}, {"id": "D", "text": "giving something up"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "giving something up". Opportunity cost is the alternative you give up.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-21', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Just-in-time factories', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'The Japanese carmaker Toyota became famous for a system called just-in-time production. Instead of keeping large stores of parts, factories received components only when they were needed. This reduced storage costs and waste, but it also made production vulnerable, because a single delay in the supply chain could quickly bring the factory to a [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "standstill"}, {"id": "B", "text": "profit"}, {"id": "C", "text": "celebration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "new location"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "standstill". Without stored parts, a delay stops production.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-22', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Cheap bread, dear baskets', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Supermarkets sometimes sell popular items, such as bread or milk, at a price that barely covers their cost, or even at a loss. These products are called loss leaders. The idea is that customers who come in for the cheap item will also buy other goods, so the shop makes its profit on the [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "bread and milk"}, {"id": "B", "text": "rest of the shopping"}, {"id": "C", "text": "car park charges"}, {"id": "D", "text": "staff wages"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "rest of the shopping". The cheap items attract shoppers who then buy other goods.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-23', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Bulbs built to fail', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In 1924, several leading light bulb manufacturers agreed to limit the life of their bulbs to around one thousand hours, and they fined members whose bulbs lasted longer. This agreement, known as the Phoebus cartel, is often cited as an early example of planned obsolescence, the practice of designing products to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "last forever"}, {"id": "B", "text": "save energy"}, {"id": "C", "text": "wear out sooner"}, {"id": "D", "text": "cost less to make"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "wear out sooner". The cartel deliberately shortened the life of bulbs.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-24', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Brands on screen', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'When a character in a film drinks a particular brand of soft drink, the company may have paid for that moment. This is product placement, and it has grown as viewers increasingly skip traditional advertisements. Because the brand appears as part of the story rather than in a separate break, audiences are less likely to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "enjoy the film"}, {"id": "B", "text": "remember the characters"}, {"id": "C", "text": "buy a ticket"}, {"id": "D", "text": "notice that they are being advertised to"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "notice that they are being advertised to". Placement hides the advertisement inside the story.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-25', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Milk at the back', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Have you noticed that everyday essentials such as milk and eggs are often placed at the back of the supermarket? To reach them, shoppers must walk past many other products, and some of these will end up in the basket. Store layouts are carefully designed to increase [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "unplanned purchases"}, {"id": "B", "text": "delivery times"}, {"id": "C", "text": "staff numbers"}, {"id": "D", "text": "the price of milk"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "unplanned purchases". Walking past other products leads shoppers to buy things they had not planned to.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-26', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Comparing currencies with a burger', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In 1986, The Economist magazine introduced the Big Mac index as a light-hearted way of comparing currencies. Because the burger is made to a similar recipe around the world, its price in different countries should, in theory, be the same once converted. If it is much cheaper in one country, that country''s currency may be [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "overvalued"}, {"id": "B", "text": "undervalued"}, {"id": "C", "text": "illegal"}, {"id": "D", "text": "unchanged"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "undervalued". A cheaper burger suggests the currency is worth more than its exchange rate shows.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-27', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Interest on interest', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'If you invest money and leave the interest in your account, next year you earn interest not only on the original amount but also on the interest already added. Over short periods the difference is small, but over decades it becomes enormous. This is why financial advisers encourage young people to start saving [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "only after retirement"}, {"id": "B", "text": "in cash at home"}, {"id": "C", "text": "as early as possible"}, {"id": "D", "text": "when interest rates fall"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "as early as possible". Compound growth is largest over long periods, so starting early matters.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-28', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'More tickets than seats', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Airlines regularly sell more tickets than there are seats on a flight. They do this because, on average, a certain number of passengers do not turn up. Using past data, they can predict how many will be missing. When their prediction is wrong, some passengers are offered compensation to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "upgrade their seats"}, {"id": "B", "text": "pay extra fees"}, {"id": "C", "text": "cancel their holiday"}, {"id": "D", "text": "take a later flight"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "take a later flight". When too many passengers arrive, some are paid to travel later.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-29', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Pooling risk', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Insurance works by pooling risk. Thousands of people each pay a relatively small amount into a shared fund. In any given year, only a few of them will suffer an expensive event, such as a house fire, and the fund pays for their losses. In this way, a cost that would ruin one family is [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "shared across many"}, {"id": "B", "text": "paid by the government"}, {"id": "C", "text": "avoided completely"}, {"id": "D", "text": "doubled each year"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "shared across many". Pooling spreads each loss across all members.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-30', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Subscriptions', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Many companies now sell products as subscriptions rather than one-off purchases, from software to razors and meal kits. For businesses, subscriptions provide a steady, predictable income. However, consumer groups warn that some services make cancelling deliberately difficult, relying on the fact that many customers [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "prefer paying in cash"}, {"id": "B", "text": "forget they are still paying"}, {"id": "C", "text": "cancel on the first day"}, {"id": "D", "text": "never use the internet"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "forget they are still paying". The warning is about customers who keep paying without noticing.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-31', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Fake goods', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Counterfeit goods are copies sold under a brand name without permission. While fake handbags and watches may seem harmless, fake medicines, car parts and electrical goods can be dangerous, because they are not made to the same standards. Customs officers therefore focus particularly on counterfeit products that could [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "look fashionable"}, {"id": "B", "text": "be sold online"}, {"id": "C", "text": "put people''s safety at risk"}, {"id": "D", "text": "cost very little"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "put people''s safety at risk". The speaker contrasts harmless fakes with dangerous ones.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-32', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Printer cartridges', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Home printers are often sold at low prices, sometimes below the cost of making them. Manufacturers make their money later, through replacement ink cartridges, which can be extremely expensive. This approach is sometimes called the razor-and-blades model, because the main profit comes from [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "the first sale"}, {"id": "B", "text": "free repairs"}, {"id": "C", "text": "advertising revenue"}, {"id": "D", "text": "repeat purchases"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "repeat purchases". Profit comes from buying cartridges again and again.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-33', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Points for shoppers', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Loyalty programmes reward customers with points or discounts for returning to the same shop. But they also give companies something valuable: detailed data about what each customer buys and when. Retailers use this information to design personalised offers, so the programme benefits the shop as much as [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "the shopper"}, {"id": "B", "text": "the government"}, {"id": "C", "text": "its competitors"}, {"id": "D", "text": "the manufacturer"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "the shopper". Both the shop and the customer gain from the scheme.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-34', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Hyperinflation in 1923', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In Germany in 1923, prices rose so fast that workers were paid twice a day and rushed to spend their wages before they lost value. Some people burned banknotes for heat because they were cheaper than fuel. By the end of the year, the currency had become almost [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "impossible to print"}, {"id": "B", "text": "worthless"}, {"id": "C", "text": "valuable again"}, {"id": "D", "text": "illegal to own"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "worthless". Hyperinflation destroyed the value of money.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-35', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'When a brand becomes a word', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Some brand names become so successful that people use them for every product of that type. Escalator, aspirin and thermos all began as trademarks. When this happens, a company can lose its legal protection, which is why firms sometimes remind the public that their brand name should not be used as [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "a company logo"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a slogan"}, {"id": "C", "text": "an ordinary word"}, {"id": "D", "text": "a product price"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "an ordinary word". Protection is lost when a brand becomes a general word.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-36', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The Red Planet', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Mars is often called the Red Planet because its surface is covered in dust rich in iron oxide, the same compound that forms rust on Earth. Fine particles of this dust are carried high into the thin atmosphere, so even the sky on Mars often looks [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "deep blue"}, {"id": "B", "text": "completely black"}, {"id": "C", "text": "bright green"}, {"id": "D", "text": "pinkish or orange"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "pinkish or orange". Red dust in the air colours the Martian sky.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-37', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Saturn''s rings', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Saturn''s rings look solid from a distance, but they are made of countless pieces of ice and rock, ranging from tiny grains to chunks as large as houses. Although the rings are hundreds of thousands of kilometres wide, in many places they are remarkably thin, often only about ten metres [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "thick"}, {"id": "B", "text": "long"}, {"id": "C", "text": "wide"}, {"id": "D", "text": "away"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "thick". The contrast is between the rings'' great width and their thinness.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-38', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Finding distant planets', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Most known planets outside our solar system were discovered by the transit method. A telescope watches a star continuously, and if a planet passes in front of it, the star''s brightness drops very slightly. By measuring how much light is blocked, astronomers can estimate the planet''s [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "age"}, {"id": "B", "text": "size"}, {"id": "C", "text": "name"}, {"id": "D", "text": "orbit around the galaxy"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "size". A larger planet blocks more light, so the dip reveals its size.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-39', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Pluto reclassified', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'When Pluto was discovered in 1930, it was counted as the ninth planet. But as astronomers found more objects of similar size in the outer solar system, they had to decide what a planet really was. In 2006, the International Astronomical Union agreed a new definition, and Pluto was reclassified as a [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "moon of Neptune"}, {"id": "B", "text": "comet"}, {"id": "C", "text": "dwarf planet"}, {"id": "D", "text": "star"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "dwarf planet". The 2006 definition made Pluto a dwarf planet.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-40', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The hottest planet', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Venus is further from the Sun than Mercury, yet it is the hottest planet in the solar system, with surface temperatures high enough to melt lead. The reason is its extremely thick atmosphere of carbon dioxide, which traps heat so effectively that the surface cannot [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "warm up"}, {"id": "B", "text": "absorb sunlight"}, {"id": "C", "text": "be very hot"}, {"id": "D", "text": "cool down"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "cool down". Trapped heat stops the surface from cooling.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-41', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The far side of the Moon', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'The Moon always shows the same face to the Earth, because it rotates once on its axis in exactly the time it takes to orbit our planet. The far side is often wrongly called the dark side, but it receives just as much sunlight. It was first photographed in 1959 by a Soviet [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "space probe"}, {"id": "B", "text": "telescope on Earth"}, {"id": "C", "text": "astronaut"}, {"id": "D", "text": "weather balloon"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "space probe". The far side cannot be seen from Earth, so a probe was needed.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-42', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Halley''s prediction', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In 1705, the astronomer Edmond Halley argued that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 were in fact the same object, returning roughly every seventy-six years. He predicted it would appear again in 1758. He died before that date, but the comet returned as he had forecast, and it was named [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "after the year it appeared"}, {"id": "B", "text": "in his honour"}, {"id": "C", "text": "by a committee"}, {"id": "D", "text": "after a ship"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "in his honour". The comet carries Halley''s name because of his prediction.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-43', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The aurora', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'The northern lights, or aurora, appear when charged particles from the Sun reach the Earth. The planet''s magnetic field guides many of these particles towards the polar regions, where they collide with gases high in the atmosphere and make them glow. This is why auroras are seen most often at [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "the equator"}, {"id": "B", "text": "midday"}, {"id": "C", "text": "high latitudes"}, {"id": "D", "text": "sea level"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "high latitudes". The particles are guided towards the poles.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-44', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Eating in orbit', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'On early space missions, astronauts ate food squeezed from tubes and small cubes coated to prevent crumbs. Crumbs were a real concern, because in weightless conditions they float around the cabin and could get into equipment or an astronaut''s eyes. Bread, for example, is usually replaced on the International Space Station by [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "biscuits"}, {"id": "B", "text": "cakes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "crackers"}, {"id": "D", "text": "tortillas"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "tortillas". Tortillas produce almost no crumbs; the other foods crumble.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-45', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Telling time by shadow', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'A sundial tells the time using the shadow cast by a pointer, called a gnomon. For the dial to be accurate throughout the year, the gnomon must be angled to point towards the celestial pole, which means its angle depends on the [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "latitude of the place"}, {"id": "B", "text": "height of the owner"}, {"id": "C", "text": "colour of the dial"}, {"id": "D", "text": "season it was built"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "latitude of the place". The angle to the pole changes with latitude.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-46', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'El Niño', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Every few years, the surface waters of the central and eastern tropical Pacific Ocean become unusually warm. This event, called El Niño, shifts rainfall patterns around the world, bringing heavy rain to parts of South America and drought to parts of Australia. Its name, meaning the boy child, refers to its tendency to appear around [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "midsummer"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Christmas"}, {"id": "C", "text": "the new moon"}, {"id": "D", "text": "the spring harvest"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "Christmas". The boy child refers to Christmas, when the warming often appears off South America.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-47', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'How hailstones grow', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Hailstones form inside powerful thunderstorm clouds. Strong upward currents of air carry water droplets high into the cloud, where they freeze. The small ice pellets fall, collect more water, and are lifted again, growing layer by layer. Hailstones fall to the ground when they become too heavy for the [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "rain to carry"}, {"id": "B", "text": "ground to absorb"}, {"id": "C", "text": "updrafts to support"}, {"id": "D", "text": "sun to melt"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (C) is correct: the recording ends with "updrafts to support". Rising air holds the stones up until they are too heavy.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-48', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'The truth about quicksand', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'In films, characters caught in quicksand often sink until they disappear. In reality, quicksand is a mixture of sand and water, and it is much denser than the human body. Researchers have shown that a person would sink only to about the waist. The real danger comes from being trapped and unable to [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "float on the surface"}, {"id": "B", "text": "see the sky"}, {"id": "C", "text": "feel their feet"}, {"id": "D", "text": "pull themselves free"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (D) is correct: the recording ends with "pull themselves free". People do not sink completely; the risk is being stuck.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-49', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Naming storms', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Tropical storms in the Atlantic are given names from lists prepared in advance by the World Meteorological Organization. Short, familiar names are easier to remember and reduce confusion when several storms are active. When a storm is particularly deadly or destructive, its name is [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "retired and replaced"}, {"id": "B", "text": "used again the next year"}, {"id": "C", "text": "given to a city"}, {"id": "D", "text": "kept secret"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (A) is correct: the recording ends with "retired and replaced". Names of very destructive storms are not reused.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-smw-50', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Meteor showers', 'You will hear a recording. At the end of the recording the last word or group of words has been replaced by a beep. Select the correct option to complete the recording.', NULL, 'Meteor showers happen when the Earth passes through a trail of dust and small particles left behind by a comet. These particles strike the atmosphere at high speed and burn up, creating streaks of light. Because the Earth crosses the same trails at the same point in its orbit each year, many showers occur [beep]', 7, 90, '[{"id": "A", "text": "only once a century"}, {"id": "B", "text": "on the same dates every year"}, {"id": "C", "text": "at random times"}, {"id": "D", "text": "during total eclipses"}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Option (B) is correct: the recording ends with "on the same dates every year". The Earth meets the same trails at the same time each year.', 'medium', 1, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Select Missing Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hiw-01', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-incorrect-word', 'Highlight Incorrect Word', 'Measles and herd immunity', 'You will hear a recording. Below is a transcription of the recording. Some words in the transcription differ from what the speaker said. Please click on the words that are different.', 'Measles is one of the most dangerous diseases known. When a high proportion of a community is vaccinated, the virus struggles to find new hosts, and even people who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies, are infected. This is called herd immunity. For measles, experts estimate that about ninety-five percent of people need to be immune, one of the lowest thresholds of any disease.', 'Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known. When a high proportion of a community is vaccinated, the virus struggles to find new hosts, and even people who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies, are protected. This is called herd immunity. For measles, experts estimate that about ninety-five percent of people need to be immune, one of the highest thresholds of any disease.', 10, 120, '[{"id": "w1", "text": "Measles"}, {"id": "w2", "text": "is"}, {"id": "w3", "text": "one"}, {"id": "w4", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w5", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w6", "text": "most"}, {"id": "w7", "text": "dangerous"}, {"id": "w8", "text": "diseases"}, {"id": "w9", "text": "known."}, {"id": "w10", "text": "When"}, {"id": "w11", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w12", "text": "high"}, {"id": "w13", "text": "proportion"}, {"id": "w14", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w15", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w16", "text": "community"}, {"id": "w17", "text": "is"}, {"id": "w18", "text": "vaccinated,"}, {"id": "w19", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w20", "text": "virus"}, {"id": "w21", "text": "struggles"}, {"id": "w22", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w23", "text": "find"}, {"id": "w24", "text": "new"}, {"id": "w25", "text": "hosts,"}, {"id": "w26", "text": "and"}, {"id": "w27", "text": "even"}, {"id": "w28", "text": "people"}, {"id": "w29", "text": "who"}, {"id": "w30", "text": "cannot"}, {"id": "w31", "text": "be"}, {"id": "w32", "text": "vaccinated,"}, {"id": "w33", "text": "such"}, {"id": "w34", "text": "as"}, {"id": "w35", "text": "newborn"}, {"id": "w36", "text": "babies,"}, {"id": "w37", "text": "are"}, {"id": "w38", "text": "infected."}, {"id": "w39", "text": "This"}, {"id": "w40", "text": "is"}, {"id": "w41", "text": "called"}, {"id": "w42", "text": "herd"}, {"id": "w43", "text": "immunity."}, {"id": "w44", "text": "For"}, {"id": "w45", "text": "measles,"}, {"id": "w46", "text": "experts"}, {"id": "w47", "text": "estimate"}, {"id": "w48", "text": "that"}, {"id": "w49", "text": "about"}, {"id": "w50", "text": "ninety-five"}, {"id": "w51", "text": "percent"}, {"id": "w52", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w53", "text": "people"}, {"id": "w54", "text": "need"}, {"id": "w55", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w56", "text": "be"}, {"id": "w57", "text": "immune,"}, {"id": "w58", "text": "one"}, {"id": "w59", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w60", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w61", "text": "lowest"}, {"id": "w62", "text": "thresholds"}, {"id": "w63", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w64", "text": "any"}, {"id": "w65", "text": "disease."}]'::jsonb, '["w7", "w38", "w61"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Select dangerous, infected, lowest. The speaker says contagious, protected, highest.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Incorrect Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hiw-02', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-incorrect-word', 'Highlight Incorrect Word', 'Insulin in Toronto', 'You will hear a recording. Below is a transcription of the recording. Some words in the transcription differ from what the speaker said. Please click on the words that are different.', 'Before 1922, a diagnosis of type one diabetes was usually temporary within a few years. In 1921, researchers in Toronto extracted insulin from the pancreas and showed that it lowered blood pressure. The following year, a fourteen-year-old boy became the first patient to receive an injection, and his condition improved slightly. The discovery earned a Nobel Prize in 1923.', 'Before 1922, a diagnosis of type one diabetes was usually fatal within a few years. In 1921, researchers in Toronto extracted insulin from the pancreas and showed that it lowered blood sugar. The following year, a fourteen-year-old boy became the first patient to receive an injection, and his condition improved dramatically. The discovery earned a Nobel Prize in 1923.', 10, 120, '[{"id": "w1", "text": "Before"}, {"id": "w2", "text": "1922,"}, {"id": "w3", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w4", "text": "diagnosis"}, {"id": "w5", "text": "of"}, {"id": "w6", "text": "type"}, {"id": "w7", "text": "one"}, {"id": "w8", "text": "diabetes"}, {"id": "w9", "text": "was"}, {"id": "w10", "text": "usually"}, {"id": "w11", "text": "temporary"}, {"id": "w12", "text": "within"}, {"id": "w13", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w14", "text": "few"}, {"id": "w15", "text": "years."}, {"id": "w16", "text": "In"}, {"id": "w17", "text": "1921,"}, {"id": "w18", "text": "researchers"}, {"id": "w19", "text": "in"}, {"id": "w20", "text": "Toronto"}, {"id": "w21", "text": "extracted"}, {"id": "w22", "text": "insulin"}, {"id": "w23", "text": "from"}, {"id": "w24", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w25", "text": "pancreas"}, {"id": "w26", "text": "and"}, {"id": "w27", "text": "showed"}, {"id": "w28", "text": "that"}, {"id": "w29", "text": "it"}, {"id": "w30", "text": "lowered"}, {"id": "w31", "text": "blood"}, {"id": "w32", "text": "pressure."}, {"id": "w33", "text": "The"}, {"id": "w34", "text": "following"}, {"id": "w35", "text": "year,"}, {"id": "w36", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w37", "text": "fourteen-year-old"}, {"id": "w38", "text": "boy"}, {"id": "w39", "text": "became"}, {"id": "w40", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w41", "text": "first"}, {"id": "w42", "text": "patient"}, {"id": "w43", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w44", "text": "receive"}, {"id": "w45", "text": "an"}, {"id": "w46", "text": "injection,"}, {"id": "w47", "text": "and"}, {"id": "w48", "text": "his"}, {"id": "w49", "text": "condition"}, {"id": "w50", "text": "improved"}, {"id": "w51", "text": "slightly."}, {"id": "w52", "text": "The"}, {"id": "w53", "text": "discovery"}, {"id": "w54", "text": "earned"}, {"id": "w55", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w56", "text": "Nobel"}, {"id": "w57", "text": "Prize"}, {"id": "w58", "text": "in"}, {"id": "w59", "text": "1923."}]'::jsonb, '["w11", "w32", "w51"]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Select temporary, pressure, slightly. The speaker says fatal, sugar, dramatically.', 'medium', 3, ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Highlight Incorrect Word']::text[], true),
    ('q-pl90-hiw-03', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE']::text[], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-incorrect-word', 'Highlight Incorrect Word', 'Allbutt''s thermometer', 'You will hear a recording. Below is a transcription of the recording. Some words in the transcription differ from what the speaker said. Please click on the words that are different.', 'Early medical thermometers were about a metre long and could take twenty minutes to give a reading. In 1867, the English doctor Thomas Allbutt designed a much heavier thermometer that took only about five minutes. This made it practical for doctors to measure temperature routinely, helping them track the progress of injuries in their patients.', 'Early medical thermometers were about a foot long and could take twenty minutes to give a reading. In 1867, the English doctor Thomas Allbutt designed a much shorter thermometer that took only about five minutes. This made it practical for doctors to measure temperature routinely, helping them track the progress of fevers in their patients.', 10, 120, '[{"id": "w1", "text": "Early"}, {"id": "w2", "text": "medical"}, {"id": "w3", "text": "thermometers"}, {"id": "w4", "text": "were"}, {"id": "w5", "text": "about"}, {"id": "w6", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w7", "text": "metre"}, {"id": "w8", "text": "long"}, {"id": "w9", "text": "and"}, {"id": "w10", "text": "could"}, {"id": "w11", "text": "take"}, {"id": "w12", "text": "twenty"}, {"id": "w13", "text": "minutes"}, {"id": "w14", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w15", "text": "give"}, {"id": "w16", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w17", "text": "reading."}, {"id": "w18", "text": "In"}, {"id": "w19", "text": "1867,"}, {"id": "w20", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w21", "text": "English"}, {"id": "w22", "text": "doctor"}, {"id": "w23", "text": "Thomas"}, {"id": "w24", "text": "Allbutt"}, {"id": "w25", "text": "designed"}, {"id": "w26", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w27", "text": "much"}, {"id": "w28", "text": "heavier"}, {"id": "w29", "text": "thermometer"}, {"id": "w30", "text